      'prompt_content' => '## Phase 3 — Model sheet view descriptions

You write one English design description for each model sheet view of a finished yacht. An image tool will receive, for each view, your description followed by a fixed camera line and a fixed studio line that the pipeline appends. You never write the camera line or the studio line.

### Input

The user message is one JSON object:

- `design` — the complete Phase 3 design answer exactly as it was stored. Read all of it. Every fact you write must come from it.
- `design_ref` — the stored design\'s id and hashes. Do not repeat them.
- `state` — always `finished`: the yacht is complete, painted, glazed and afloat-ready. Use the finished surfaces; never show build, primer, film or scaffold states.
- `views` — the views to write, in order. Each has `id` and `camera`, the fixed camera line that will follow your description. Use `camera` only to decide which parts are visible from that view and on which side of the frame they sit.
- `coverage_paths` — design paths that every view must account for, either as evidence of what the description shows or as a part not shown with a reason. This list is for accounting only; it is not the limit of what you read or use.

### What each description contains

Write plain descriptive prose for the image tool, in this order of importance:

1. Overall shape and proportions: length, deck count, how the decks sit inside or above the outer envelope, the profile line from bow to stern.
2. The parts visible from this view: position along the length, count, relative size, side (port or starboard) and deck.
3. The identity features (innovations) visible from this view, with their exact position and form.
4. Materials and colours: each colour by its design name followed by its HEX code, assigned to the right part.
5. Design prohibitions that apply to the visible area, stated as absences ("no freestanding mast", "no helideck").
6. Clarification of terms an image tool is likely to misread. Rewrite the term instead of repeating it:
   - Static geometry must never be described with motion words. Ribs or corrugations whose direction differs along the hull "change direction by position"; never call them rotating, turning, spinning or twisting.
   - A colour name that suggests a different hue gets its HEX code and a plain hue word ("Oxidized Copper #547A76, a muted blue-green, not orange copper").
   - Decks contained inside one continuous envelope are not stepped or stacked tiers; say so when the design says so.
   - Equipment heads on pedestals, such as mirror or sensor heads, are not masts; give their size relative to the hull and their exact position.
   - A slot, canyon or opening states its orientation (lengthwise or crosswise), where it starts and ends, and whether it is sealed or open.
   - Terraces, pools and bars state which recess or face they sit in.

### Rules

- Every fact comes from `design`. Do not invent parts, counts, sizes, colours or positions. Do not resolve contradictions between design fields: report them in `findings`.
- Write measured values with digits and their unit as the design gives them ("150 m", "21.8 m", "7 decks"). Use a number only if it appears in a field you cite in `evidence` for that view.
- Write HEX codes exactly as the design gives them, each after its colour name.
- Port is positive y and starboard is negative y in the design\'s coordinate system. When you place a part on one side, cite the field that gives its side.
- Do not write camera, lens, framing, viewpoint, background, studio or lighting words. The pipeline appends those.
- Do not use a mention of a forbidden part except as an absence ("no", "without").
- Describe only what the camera of that view can show. Parts hidden from the view go in `not_shown` with a reason; do not describe them.
- Each description is self-contained prose without lists, headings, quotation marks, field names or paths.

### Evidence

For each view list the design fields the description relies on.

- `path` is a dot path from the root of `design`, with list positions as numbers, for example `data.yacht_bible.innovations.0.position` or `data.canonical.landmarks.heliostat_port`.
