- Name each measured value by the property of the part it belongs to ("canyon width", "beam", "crest height"); do not give the value of one part or property to another.
- Do not write camera, lens, framing, viewpoint, background, studio or lighting words. The pipeline appends those.
- Do not use a mention of a forbidden part except as an absence ("no", "without").
- Describe only what the camera of that view can show. Parts hidden from the view go in `not_shown` with a reason; do not describe them.
- Each description is self-contained prose without lists, headings, quotation marks, field names or paths.

### Evidence

For each view, link every part of the description to the design field it states.

- `text` is copied character for character from your own description: the phrase or clause that states this fact. Every clause of the description (a part separated by a comma, semicolon or colon, or introduced by and, with, while, but, plus, whereas, which, that or where) must contain at least one evidence `text`.
- `path` is a dot path from the root of `design`, with list positions as numbers, for example `data.yacht_bible.innovations.0.position` or `data.canonical.landmarks.heliostat_port`. `data` holds four sibling branches, and each path starts with exactly one of them: `data.yacht_bible.…`, `data.anchors.…` (for example `data.anchors.exterior.EXT01` or `data.anchors.finished_surface`), `data.build_states.…` and `data.canonical.…`. `anchors` is not inside `yacht_bible`. Copy every key exactly as it appears in `design`; a path that does not exist in `design` fails the check. Cite the field of the exact part and property the `text` describes: a length cites the length field, a beam cites the beam field, a port mirror cites the port mirror.
- `quote` is copied character for character from the value at that path. For a field whose value is a number or an object, quote the exact text of one of its leaf values (for example `150` or `#344C59`).
- `side` is optional: `port`, `starboard` or `centreline`, given only when the `text` places that part on a side and the cited field carries a `y` coordinate.
- Every number and every HEX code in the description must sit inside an evidence `text` whose cited field holds that same value with the same unit and sign.

Every path in `coverage_paths` must appear, for every view, either as an evidence path (or the parent of one) or in `not_shown` with a reason specific to that view. A person reviews every `not_shown` entry before approving the description; a part that the camera can see must not be listed there.

### Findings

Report, for each view, anything that stops the description from being exact:

- `conflict` — two design fields disagree about something this view shows.
- `gap` — the design does not state something this view must show.
- `ambiguity` — a design term could be drawn in more than one way.

Set `severity` to `blocking` when the view cannot be drawn correctly until a person decides, otherwise `note`. Name the paths involved.

### Output

Return only this JSON object, with one entry per requested view, in the requested order:

```json
{
  "views": [
    {
      "view_id": "MS1",
      "description": "English prose for the image tool.",
      "evidence": [
        {"text": "no freestanding mast", "path": "data.yacht_bible.exterior.mast_and_radar", "quote": "There is no freestanding mast.", "side": null}
      ],
      "not_shown": [
        {"path": "data.yacht_bible.innovations.2", "reason": "Why this view cannot show it."}
      ],
      "findings": [
        {"severity": "note", "kind": "ambiguity", "paths": ["data.yacht_bible.innovations.1.geometry"], "message": "What is unclear and why."}
      ]
    }
  ]
}
```
