- `design` — the complete stored Phase 3 answer. Read all of it. Every fact you write must come from it or from `dna`. Interior branches are there only to understand exterior openings, glazing, voids and outer boundaries.
- `dna` — the Phase 2 design DNA the design was built from: innovations, departures, must-avoid items.
- `facts` — every numeric value of the design, one per line: `id | path | property | render`. `render` is the exact text code prints for that fact; `property` is the field it measures.
- `entities` — parts code located: landmarks `L:…`, ranged items `R:…`, innovations `N:…`, with `side` and `side_source` (declared, coordinates or unknown — unknown is not centre), `level` and `level_source` (declared or estimated), `from`/`to` (fraction of length from the bow; empty when the design gives no number), `full_beam`, `facing` (only when the design declares it), `near_side` (only a hint that the point lies close to one side), `pair` (the mirrored part, matched by coordinates) and their `facts`.
- `views` — MS1–MS8 in order: `label`, `camera` (the camera text code will print), `sees_only` (the only side that camera sees, if any), `detail_view` and `candidates` (entities to decide for that view, chosen by position priorities).
- `ms8` — the structured detail target: `innovation`, whether the design `declared` it in its own MS8 label, its `path` and `range` when known.
- `occluders` — named envelope parts and exterior zones (`P:hull`, `P:superstructure`, `P:EXT05`…) with their paths; with the entities, these are the only parts that can hide another part.
- `state_sources` — the design\'s operational limits, each with a stable id `OL:<feature>`, its path and its fallback state text.
- `coverage` — items that must be accounted for: innovations, identity features, must-avoid resolutions and state sources.
- `source_conflicts` — contradictions code found in the design itself (empty when none).
- `draft` — what code would have sent without you. Compare against it and report your changes.
- `negatives_target` — the soft limit for critical absences.

### References and numbers

Every sentence has `text`, `refs` and `numbers`. `refs` lists the entity ids (`N:N1`, `L:crest`), fact ids or design paths it relies on. Copy a design path exactly as the keys appear in `design` or `dna`, with list positions as numbers — for example the exterior anchors are under `data.anchors.exterior.EXT01`, not under `data.canonical`. Never guess a path; if you cannot find the key, cite the entity or fact instead. Name an entity in `refs` whenever the sentence describes that part. Code resolves every reference to the part that owns it: a fact id or a path inside an entity\'s own data (for example `data.canonical.landmarks.crest.z` or `data.yacht_bible.innovations.0.position`) counts as citing that entity.

Never write digits. Write a fact token `{{F12}}`; code prints that fact\'s `render` text in its place, so write the sentence to read correctly with it: "a sealed slot {{F8}} wide" prints "a sealed slot 3 m wide"; "at {{F95}}" prints "at 70% of the length from the bow"; a height fact prints "21.8 metres above the design waterline". Identifiers such as N1, MS8 or EXT02 are allowed. Write counts as words ("two mirror units", "seven decks").

For every token add one `numbers` entry `{fact, subject, property}`:

- `fact` — the token\'s fact id.
- `subject` — the part the number belongs to: the entity id whose own data holds the fact (`L:heliostat_port` for its coordinates), the innovation id for one of its parameters (`N:N1` for `canyon_width`), `yacht` for whole-yacht quantities (dimensions, specs, decks, outlines, derived values), or the design path of the record that holds the fact.
- `property` — exactly the fact\'s `property` column.

Use each quantity for what it measures: a landmark height is the height of that point; only a floor field is a floor height; the beam is not the width of a part. A token without its `numbers` entry, or with a subject that does not own the fact, is rejected.

### What to write

1. `shared` — the invariant identity that every panel draws, in this order: type and overall proportions; how the decks sit relative to the outer envelope (the total deck count is not a count of exposed tiers); the profile from bow to stern with positions; the plan shape, including the stern width when a fact gives it; every innovation — what it is, its size and its position; every asymmetric part with its side. Bring in the identity sizes the facts give (widths, lengths, diameters, counts, pool or terrace sizes and heights). Prefer exact quantities to adjectives such as narrow, shallow or recessed.
2. `surfaces` — colours by name and role (no hex codes), glazing, coatings and metal finishes.
3. `display_state` — one entry per visible movable or reconfigurable part: `part_id`, `part` (its name), `state` (the single state shown across the whole sheet), `refs`, `numbers`. For a part governed by a state source use its id as `part_id` (`OL:canyon_heliostat_active`) and include that source\'s path in `refs`. Use `other:<short-name>` only for a part with no state source, and then cite the design text that states its displayed state. Never show an active effect such as a beam or moving light unless the design states it as the displayed static state. A state source with no visible configuration of a part (draining a tank, closing access, isolating a system) gets no display state: account for it in `coverage` as not_visible or not_exterior.
4. `views` — exactly MS1 to MS8 in order.
   - `sentences`: what this camera adds about this yacht — the parts it sees, on which side and where along the length — without repeating `shared`. Mention only parts this camera sees: every entity your sentences cite, directly or through its facts or paths, must have the decision `include` in that view, and a hidden part never appears in `sentences` (the image model reads every sentence as something to draw). For a part that has a mirrored `pair`, cite the one on the side this camera sees; a part whose declared `facing` is the other side is never cited in that view. Never ask for a part to be moved, enlarged, duplicated or made visible through another part.
   - `decisions`: one per candidate, plus one for every other entity your sentences cite. `include` — a sentence of this view cites the entity. `shared` — a `shared` sentence cites it and the view adds nothing. `occluded` — set `basis` to the part that hides it: an entity id (or one of its paths) or an `occluders` id (or its path). A colour, a dimension, a parameter or any other text is not a part and is rejected. `out_of_frame` — only in a `detail_view`; whole-yacht panels contain the complete yacht. `state_hidden` — set `basis` to the `part_id` of the display state that hides it. `not_exterior` or `unknown` — explain in `note`. Otherwise `basis` is null. Keep `note` empty for `include` and `shared`, and at most a few words elsewhere: the decisions list is long and every word of it costs output. The positions that produced the candidates are priorities, not evidence: "aft of 60% of the length" is not a reason for a part to be hidden. Respect `sees_only` and declared `facing`.
5. `ms8_target` — the innovation MS8 shows: always `ms8.innovation`, because the camera text code prints is built from it. If that target does not suit a detail view, keep it and report a `camera` finding instead of choosing another innovation. List in `entities` the landmarks that belong to it (required unless `ms8.range` is known) and put in `refs` the path of that innovation\'s own text in `design` or `dna` that places those landmarks on it. Do not attach a landmark only because it is nearby.
6. `critical_absences` — short absences that protect identity decisions, for example "no freestanding mast". Each appears once, with `refs`. Aim for no more than `negatives_target`; turn other prohibitions into positive descriptions.
7. `coverage` — every coverage item exactly once. `used`: `where` lists the blocks (`shared`, `surfaces`, `display_state`, `critical_absences`) or view ids that carry it, and at least one sentence there must cite it — the innovation entity, or a path or fact inside the item\'s path. A state source is `used` only through a display state with the same `part_id`. `not_exterior` or `not_visible`: `where` is empty and `note` says why.
8. `changes` — what you added, removed, merged or rewrote compared with `draft`, and why: interior content, non-visual operation or study, duplicate, negation rewritten, missing identity size added, and so on.
9. `findings` — `conflict` (two design fields disagree about something the image must show), `gap` (an identity part lacks the data to draw it correctly), `ambiguity` (a term can be drawn more than one way) or `camera` (a camera cannot show what it is meant to show; you may cite view ids). Severity `blocking` when the image cannot be drawn correctly until a person fixes the design, otherwise `note`. Name the refs involved.

### Rules

- Do not design. If the design lacks or contradicts geometry that the image needs, report it; never invent a size, position or part to make the text look complete. A part the design states does not exist is not missing data.
- Leave out interior rooms, interior materials and lighting, operating procedures, engineering studies and regulations unless they fix an exterior shape.
- Keep port and starboard exactly as the coordinate system defines them (y positive is port). Do not assume symmetry the design does not state.
- Say each thing once, in the block where it belongs. Compact sentences; no quality adjectives.
- Return only the JSON object.
