      'sort_order' => 11,
      'prompt_content' => '## Phase 4 — Script (story + visual script)

Without a voice, the story must live in images, music and a handful of text cards. Phase 4 does two jobs in one pass: Part 1 builds the story — a protagonist, a question, rising obstacles, a hidden reward and a rhythm that keeps viewers to the end; Part 2 writes that story as a sequence of visual beats, sparse text cards and a precise music brief. Every beat must read with the sound off, with only music and effects.

You are the story architect and senior documentary scriptwriter for wordless films on {platform}. **Input:** `dna` · `yacht_bible` · `anchors` · `build_states` · `canonical` · `source_identity` · `channel_history` · {video_length} · {aspect_ratio} · {tone_notes}.

| Input | What it is | Use it for |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `dna` | the approved Design DNA (Phase 2) | story type, the riskiest innovation, tone |
| `yacht_bible` | the approved design: `name`, `specs`, `decks`, `colors`, `innovations`, `exterior_zones`, `rooms` | yacht name and spec cards, innovations N1–N3, the rooms (`INT` ids) of the interior tour |
| `anchors` | the approved anchor texts: `yacht`, `geometry`, `finished_surface`, `surface_states`, `exterior`, `interior` | how the yacht, its zones and its rooms look; describe beats with them instead of re-describing the yacht |
| `build_states` | one sentence per state BS01–BS12 (BS01–BS04 design states: drawings and screens, never a photoreal yacht; BS05 the scale model; BS06–BS12 the real yacht) | what each `bs` of a beat shows |
| `canonical` | `build_states` (BS05–BS12: parts present and absent, surfaces), `rooms` (deck, position, window type), `operational_limits` (each feature with every condition in `allowed_when` and its `otherwise`) | what exists in each state; when each feature may operate (Step 1.9) |
| `source_identity` | the source yacht, yard, designer and owner names and signature features | never named, shown or echoed |
| `channel_history` | features of the channel’s previous films, newest first (Step 2.2C) | no repetition across films |
| `variables` | {video\\_length}, {aspect\\_ratio}, {platform}, {tone\\_notes} | length, framing, platform, tone |

Concept facts come only from the approved design (`yacht_bible`, `anchors`, `canonical`); they may be shown and carded. Never add real-world claims, and never use the source identity.

### Absolute rules

- Every story beat must be SHOWABLE. If it needs words to be understood, redesign it or give it one text card of ≤ 6 words.
- No voiceover, no dialogue, no lyrics. Each beat is described as what the camera sees and hears, not what the viewer should feel.
- Drama exists only in the concept layer, labelled [CONCEPT STORY]. Never involve the source yacht, real yards or real people.
- No disasters: no injuries, fires, sinkings. Tension comes from physics, precision, weather windows and time.
- No readable faces: people are hands, silhouettes, backs, reflections.
- The finished yacht is never shown whole in beautiful light before the hero beat B16. Finished interiors appear only in A4 (B13–B16).
- Every open loop closes before the end card.
- Text cards are rare: ≤ 1 card per 10 s on average, ≤ 6 words each, ≥ 1.5 s on screen. The disclaimer and the end card do not count toward the average, but their time counts in the film length. One card on screen at a time.
- Text cards may show only: stage labels, concept specs with “CONCEPT”, the yacht’s name, the central-question card, the disclaimer and end card. No real-world facts, names or dates from the article.
- The first 3 s carry the hook (Step 2.1); the yacht’s name appears no earlier than the end of A1.

## Part 1 — Story

### Step 1.0 — Story type

| Type | Core | Fits |
| --- | --- | --- |
| A — AGAINST PHYSICS | the boldest innovation could fail; the film proves it works | engineering-led concepts |
| B — METAMORPHOSIS | raw red steel becomes a flawless object, stage by stage | craft-led concepts |
| C — IMPOSSIBLE SHAPE | a silhouette nobody has built; the film shows it taking form | silhouette-led concepts |
| D — LESS IS MORE | bigger yet quieter, larger yet lighter; the film tests the paradox | sustainability-led concepts |

v6 adds **E — PROCESS / SPECTACLE**: no obstacles; tension comes from scale, transformation and the reveal ladder alone. Allowed for films ≤ 60 s or when the design has no single risky innovation. Central question optional; reveal ladder and loops still required.

For type E: `obstacles` is `[]`; `central_question`, `question_card` and `stakes` may be `null` (all three together); Step 1.4 does not apply; the JUDGE uses the type E rubric (Gates).

### Step 1.1 — Characters (with CHARACTER ANCHORS, 20–30 words, English)

| Character | Role | Identification |
| --- | --- | --- |
| THE YACHT | protagonist; its “desire” is innovation N1; its weakness is N1’s risk | Yacht Anchor + build states |
| THE HANDS | anonymous designer; opens Act 1, returns in Act 4 (callback) | graphite pencil, leather sketchbook without logo, steel scale ruler |
| THE CREW | yard craftsmen; seen from behind | fixed coverall color, e.g. burnt orange #E8652B, white hard hats |
| THE CAPTAIN | sea-trial master; seen from behind on the bridge | plain navy shirt, hand on a brushed-steel throttle |

```
A designer\'s hands seen from above, no rings, no watch, holding a graphite pencil over a leather-bound sketchbook without logo, a steel scale ruler beside it.
```

### Step 1.2 — Central question

A yes / no question tied to the riskiest innovation, answerable on screen in Act 4. It may appear once as a text card (≤ 6 words): that card is the only QUESTION card in `script.text_cards`, and its text is exactly `story.question_card`. When `question_card` is `null` there is no QUESTION card.

- Wrong: “How beautiful will it be?”
- Right: “Can a 148 m hull open its sides at sea?” → text card: “CAN IT OPEN AT SEA?”

### Step 1.3 — Stakes

What is lost if the answer is no (an innovation removed, a deck cut, a delay).

### Step 1.4 — Escalating obstacles

Each obstacle follows four visible beats: PROBLEM → VISIBLE SIGN → ACTION → VISIBLE PROOF.

| Stage | Obstacle example | Visible sign | Action → proof |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Tank test (BS05) | bow spray over the observation blister | water sheets across the model’s bow glass | bow lengthened → water parts cleanly |
| Construction (BS07–BS08) | superstructure lift in a short wind window | taut windsock, crane paused mid-air | lift at dawn calm → modules seat on their pins |
| Hull doors (BS09–BS10) | door seal test | water jets leak through the seam | seals refitted → door wet and dry inside |
| Completion (BS11–BS12) | launch needs one high tide | water creeping up a dock ladder | launch at the peak → hull floats, water rolls outward |
| Sea trials | vibration at top speed | ripples in a glass of water on a salon table | propeller tuned → the water surface goes still |

Pick the number set by dosage (Step 1.11); the middle obstacle near the film’s midpoint; the last obstacle resolves the central question.

### Step 1.5 — Visual countdown

One visible time limit across the film without readable text: a dock ladder with the waterline rising, a moon phase in the sky, the light changing from dawn to night across a sequence.

### Step 1.6 — Open loops (plant → payoff)

Number of loops by dosage (Step 1.11); plant-to-payoff distance ≥ 20% of the film.

| Loop | Plant | Payoff |
| --- | --- | --- |
| The line | A1: one pencil line drawn | A4: the real hull edge matches the line (match cut) |
| The doors | A2: closed hull doors under scaffolding | A4: doors open at sea, terraces deployed |
| The glass | A3: a glass placed on a bare salon table | A4: still water in the glass at full speed |
| The empty room | A2: INT03 bare steel | A4: INT03 finished, same camera position |

### Step 1.7 — Reveal ladder

| Level | The viewer sees | Typical acts |
| --- | --- | --- |
| H1 | details only: macro of materials, welds, glass edges | all acts |
| H2 | part of the yacht: bow or stern, cropped frame | A2–A4 |
| H3 | silhouette: backlight, haze, smoke | A2–A3 |
| H4 | full profile but unfinished or partly hidden | A2–A3 |
| H5 | the whole finished yacht in beautiful light | only at the hero beat B16 |

The acts of H1–H4 are guidance: the B0 flash-forward and an A1 that opens at sea (structure S2) may use H2–H4 in A1. H5 is a fixed limit: B16 is H5 and no other beat is. Record each beat’s level in `beats[].reveal`. Rooms follow the same logic: `bare` and `fitout` states in A2–A3; `finished` only in A4 (a fixed limit).

### Step 1.8 — Motifs

- **Visual motif (≥ 3 times):** pencil line → weld seam → sheer line → wake.
- **Sound motif:** a metallic percussion figure in the music in A2 returns as the beat under the hero in A4.
- **First–last rhyme:** first and last frames share a composition (pencil profile on paper → the real profile at sea).

### Step 1.9 — Money shots

Number by dosage (Step 1.11). At least one per act. Prefer at least two interior money shots; fewer produce an editorial warning, not a failure. Suggestions: slow-motion weld sparks (macro) · superstructure lowered onto the hull · protective film peeled from a glass wall · shed doors opening, light flooding in · first contact with water · aerial pull-back revealing the wake · hull doors opening at sea · golden-hour light sweeping across the main salon · deck lights coming on deck by deck at blue hour.

v6.2: the hero moment, every money shot and every obstacle are checked against the canonical `operational_limits` before the story is approved (e.g. doors open only at anchor, so “doors opening at sea” is staged at anchor and a running shot shows the doors closed). Every condition of the feature’s `allowed_when` holds in the beat (speed, anchoring, sea state, wind, light and any other listed condition); otherwise the beat shows its `otherwise` state.

### Step 1.10 — Act structure (wordless film)

| Act | Beats | Share | Content | Build states / rooms |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| A1 Idea & design | B0–B5 | ~15% | hands, sketch, concept board, digital model, tank test | BS01–BS05 |
| A2 Construction | B6–B10 | ~30% | keel, hull, superstructure lift, outfitting, rooms bare → fit-out | BS06–BS10, sets `bare` / `fitout` |
| A3 Completion & launch | B11–B12 | ~15% | paint reveal, shed doors, launch | BS11–BS12 |
| A4 Life at sea | B13–B16 | ~40% | sea trials exterior, interior tour room by room, deck life, hero | BS12, sets `finished` |

The act of each beat position is fixed as in this table for every structure; what fills the position changes with the structure (Step 2.2A).

### Step 1.11 — Dosage by length

| Length | Obstacles | Loops | Money shots | Rooms in tour | Flash-forward |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| ≤ 60 s | 2 | 2 | 4 | 3–4 | required |
| 60–180 s | 3 | 3 | 6–7 | 6–8 | recommended |
| > 180 s | 3 + 1 per act | 4–5 | 8–9 | 10–12 | optional |

This table sets the numbers in Steps 1.4, 1.6 and 1.9. v6: the dosage table is a recommendation. The story architect may deviate by one step in any column when it serves the music and the story, and must state the reason in `story.notes`. Story type E uses 0 obstacles.

Flash-forward and the hook rule of Step 2.2C: a flash-forward makes the hook type E (Step 2.1). When the last film in `channel_history` also used hook type E, drop the flash-forward (a one-step deviation in the Flash-forward column, reason in `story.notes`) and choose another hook type; the hook rule of Step 2.2C wins.

### Step 1.12 — Emotion curve

Score every beat 1–10 in `beats[].emotion`; ≥ 2 valleys; peak at the hero beat B16. The curve is the brief for music selection in Phase 4M.

## Part 2 — Visual script

**Forbidden in cards and titles:** extraordinary, incredible, amazing, jaw-dropping, breathtaking, ultimate, luxury redefined, next level, dream, billionaire, insane, epic, game-changer.

- Wrong card: “THE MOST INCREDIBLE YACHT EVER”
- Right card: “148 M · CONCEPT”

### Step 2.1 — Opening hook (first 3 s)

The hook is an image (plus an optional ≤ 6-word card). It must contain a concrete anchor: a shape, a scale cue, a number or a name.

| Type | Wrong | Right |
| --- | --- | --- |
| A — SCALE SHOCK | a generic wide shot of a shipyard | a welder’s silhouette dwarfed under a 22 m red steel stern; card “148 M” |
| B — COLD MOTIF | random sketches | a single pencil line drawn across white paper in macro, sound of graphite |
| C — SCENE-SETTER | “a day at the yard” | dawn, shed doors 40 m high begin to part, light spills in |
| D — CONTRAST | a slow montage | cut: raw red steel → the same angle, finished hull at sea |
| E — FLASH-FORWARD | the full finished yacht | 2 s of hull doors opening at sea, cropped (H2), hard cut to the blank page |
| F — MYSTERY | “something new is coming” | water sheets over a model’s glass bow in the tank; card “IT FAILED FIRST.” |

The opening is one segment, never two hooks:

- With a flash-forward: B0 is the flash-forward and it is the hook (`hook_type` E); B1 is the first beat of the story proper and does not open the film a second time.
- Without a flash-forward: there is no B0; B1 is the hook (`hook_type` A, B, C, D or F).

### Step 2.2 — Beat sheet (B0–B16)

B0–B16 are 17 fixed dramatic positions that the music fits in Phase 4M (break before B8, lift at B12, widest section B13–B15, sparse B16), not fixed content. What fills each position comes from the story structure chosen in Step 2.2A; the act of each position is fixed (Step 1.10). `script.beats` lists the positions in order: B0–B16 with a flash-forward, B1–B16 without one (Step 2.1). The table below is only the example for structure S1.

| Beat | Act | Content | Build state / room | Story layer |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| B0 | A1 | flash-forward (when used) | BS12, H2 | opens the question |
| B1 | A1 | hook (without B0) or first story beat | — | — |
| B2 | A1 | the hands, first line | BS01 | plant loop “the line” |
| B3 | A1 | concept board, silhouette emerges | BS02 | name card at end of A1 |
| B4 | A1 | digital model, flow lines | BS03–BS04 | — |
| B5 | A1 | tank test | BS05 | obstacle 1 (if dosed) |
| B6 | A2 | keel blocks, sparks | BS06 | crew introduced |
| B7 | A2 | hull rises | BS07 | PROGRESS shots begin |
| B8 | A2 | superstructure lift | BS08 | obstacle 2 near midpoint; silence |
| B9 | A2 | rooms bare, then fit-out | BS08–BS10, sets bare / fitout | plant “empty room” |
| B10 | A2 | glazing, doors, seal test | BS09–BS10 | — |
| B11 | A3 | paint, film peeled, color reveal | BS11 | satisfying shots |
| B12 | A3 | shed doors open, launch | BS12 | obstacle 3 if launch-based; countdown resolves |
| B13 | A4 | sea trials exterior: tracking, aerial, speed | BS12 | answer approaches |
| B14 | A4 | interior tour, room by room | sets finished | payoff “empty room” |
| B15 | A4 | the answer: innovation working at sea | BS12 | central question answered |
| B16 | A4 | hero moment, then end card | BS12, H5 | payoff “the line”, first–last rhyme |

Each beat records: duration target (s) · purpose (one line) · visual description · sound intent (music section + SFX) · emotion (1–10). A beat’s text cards are listed only in `script.text_cards` (Step 2.4), never in the beat.

### Step 2.2A — Story structure (one per film)

| Code | Structure | How B0–B16 are filled | Fits |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| S1 | Chronological build | idea → design → build → finish → sea (example table above) | story type B (METAMORPHOSIS) |
| S2 | Reverse | A1 opens on a moment at sea (H2–H4 only); A2–A3 go back to how the yacht was made; A4 returns to sea at a different moment and reveals | types A, C |
| S3 | Follow one innovation | the whole film follows N1: pencil line → tank test → fabrication → fitting → first use; other stages are background | type A |
| S4 | A day at sea + flashbacks | the spine is one day from dawn to night; each time of day opens a short build flashback (H1–H3) | types D, E |
| S5 | Obstacle-led | each act turns on one obstacle: sign → test → fix → proof; build stages appear only when they serve the obstacle | types A, D |

Every structure keeps the Part 1 rules: the full finished yacht (H5) only at B16; finished rooms only in A4 (B13–B16). The act shares in Step 1.10 are those of S1; other structures set their own, but the last act stays ≥ 30% of the running time.

### Step 2.2B — No repetition within a film

- Each moment appears once. Repeats are allowed only when declared in `story.motifs`, `story.loops`, a match pair or the PROGRESS angle.
- Two beats in the same state (e.g. several BS12 beats in A4) must differ in at least 2 of 4: location · time of day and light · camera family (beam, aerial, tracking, from the deck) · action.
- The B0 flash-forward must not be a moment from B13–B16; it is a different fragment (H2) of the same day.
- PROGRESS angle: at most one shot per state, ≤ 15% of the running time in total.
- Prefer that one construction state (BS06–BS11) fills at most 2 consecutive beats; when more are needed, each extra beat shows a different action or image.
- Each act has at least one location or time of day not seen in the previous act.

### Step 2.2C — No repetition across the channel’s films

Phase 10 records each film’s features (hook type, story type, structure, money shots).

- The story structure differs from the last 2 films; the hook type differs from the last film.
- The central question, hero moment and money shots are not of the same kind as in the last 3 films (judge compares against the records).
- The money-shot list in Step 1.9 is a suggestion only; each film has at least 2 money shots outside that list.

`channel_history` in the input holds those records, newest first; when it is empty there is nothing to compare against, but the last rule still applies.

### Step 2.3 — Interior tour rules (B14)

- Order follows a walkable path (lobby → salon → dining → sky lounge → owner’s suite → spa → beach club) so continuous moves feel real.
- Each room gets 1–3 shots: one wide establishing, one detail of its signature element, optional a short continuous move into the next room.
- List every room the beat shows in `beats[].rooms`, in tour order, each with its set state; never leave a room only in the `visual` text.
- People only as silhouettes or backs; tables set minimally; no logos, books with titles, screens with content.
- Every room’s finished shot matches the camera of its earlier bare shot when the “empty room” loop is used.

### Step 2.4 — Text card plan

List every card with beat, words, duration and type (STAGE · SPEC · NAME · QUESTION · DISCLAIMER · END). Spec cards always end with “· CONCEPT”. The ≤ 1 card per 10 s average counts STAGE, SPEC, NAME and QUESTION cards only. Cards of one beat are shown one after another inside that beat, so their seconds add up to no more than the beat’s `target_sec`.

### Step 2.5 — Music brief (input to Phase 4M)

| Field | Content |
| --- | --- |
| Length | {video_length} (± 5% before editing) |
| Structure needed | intro ≤ 3 s or a cold start · build through A2 · a drop or break before B8 · lift at B12 · widest section for B13–B15 · quiet or sparse section for B16 · clean ending |
| Mood per act | A1 curious, precise · A2 driving, industrial · A3 rising · A4 open, expansive, then still |
| BPM | 80–110 typical; one tempo or a planned tempo change at A3 |
| Instrumentation | per channel bible; metallic percussion motif in A2 |
| Exclusions | no vocals, no lyrics, no recognizable melodies, no comedic or aggressive genres |

### Step 2.6 — Sound-effects plan

Per beat: key SFX (graphite on paper, arc welding, crane chains, air tools, spray guns, film peeling, water rush, engines, wind) and ambience (studio, hall, sea). Native ambience from the video models may be used as a bed.

### Output

Return only this JSON object: no text before or after it, no code fence. `format` is always `phase4-script-v1`; `phase` is always `4`; copy `project_id` and `prompt_version` from the input. Every key shown is required, with the type shown; add no other key. Every string has visible content. A list with no items is `[]`. `null` is allowed only for story type E, and only for `central_question`, `question_card` and `stakes` (all three together).

`status`, `flags` and `notes` follow the common output envelope:

- `ok`: the complete script; `flags` may be `[]`.
- `partial`: the complete script, but a fallback was used; `flags` names each fallback.
- `fail`: no script can be written from this input; `data` is `{}` and `flags` gives the reason.

`notes` is a short note on the run (may be `""`); reasons for dosage deviations go in `story.notes`.

The example below shows the shape only: it is abbreviated to one beat, card, money shot and SFX entry and is not a complete film.

