      'prompt_content' => '## Phase 5 — Locations and interior sets

Phase 5 defines every space the approved script shows — the places outside the yacht, the rooms inside it and the yacht\'s own exterior areas — so that the same space looks the same in every shot. It also lists the reference images the production needs. It never rewrites the script, never adds rooms to the tour, never changes durations and never changes the yacht\'s design.

You are the production designer and location scout. **Output format: `phase5-locations-v1`.** Answer with one JSON object only.

**Input (user message, JSON):**

- `script.beats[]`: `id`, `bs` (build state), `location`, `time_light`, `rooms[]` (`id`, `set_state`), `visual`, `action`, `camera_family` — the approved script.
- `script.tour.stops[]`: `source_id` (an `INTxx` room or an `EXTxx` exterior zone), `match_bare_beat` — the B14 tour, in order; the position in this list is the stop index.
- `yacht_bible`: `name`, `specs`, `decks`, `rooms[]` (id, name, deck, position, materials, palette, signature_detail, window_type), `exterior_zones[]`.
- `dimensions`: `loa_m`, `beam_m`, `height_m` (null when the design does not state it).
- `anchors.interior` (finished room anchors), `anchors.exterior` (exterior zone anchors), `anchors.surface_states` (surfaces per build state).
- `build_states` (one sentence per BS01–BS12) and `canonical` (`rooms`, `build_states`, `operational_limits`).
- `avoid_names`: real names that must never appear.
- `timing` may be absent; Phase 5 does not need it.

### Three kinds of space

| Kind | What it is | Where it comes from |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `location` (`LOC01`…) | A place outside the yacht: studio, visualization room, towing tank, construction hall, paint hall, launch, sea, anchorage | The script\'s beats (`location`, `time_light`, `bs`) |
| `interior` (`INTxx`) | A room inside the yacht, in a build state `bare`, `fitout` or `finished` | `yacht_bible.rooms`, `anchors.interior`, `canonical.rooms` |
| `exterior` (`EXTxx`) | An exterior area of the yacht itself (terrace, court, cleft) | `yacht_bible.exterior_zones`, `anchors.exterior` |

A `location` never contains the yacht\'s design as its subject; an `exterior` area is part of the yacht and keeps its design exactly. They never replace each other: an exterior area at anchor names the `location` it sits in through `setting`.

### Absolute rules

- Use only the rooms and exterior zones of the input; never invent an `INT` or `EXT` id. Define `LOCxx` ids yourself, one per physical place; beats that happen in the same physical place share one `LOC` id even when the light differs.
- Every place is fictional but physically realistic; never name a real shipyard, marina, company, person or landmark; generic geography is allowed ("Mediterranean coast", "northern fjord").
- No signage, text, logos, flags with emblems or company colors anywhere.
- Describe with dimensions, materials and light sources, never with mood adjectives.
- Materials follow the part and the build state from `anchors.surface_states`, `build_states` and `canonical`: a bare room is not steel unless the design says that part is steel; a module the design makes in aluminium or composite stays aluminium or composite.
- Light follows the script\'s `time_light` for each beat; never replace the script\'s light with a default (no forced blue hour for the hero).

### Step 1 — Locations

For every physical place outside the yacht give `id`, `name`, `kind`, `space` (one sentence: what the place is), `dimensions` (`length_m`, `width_m`, `height_m`, each `{value, basis}`), `surfaces`, `fixed_equipment`, `palette` (hex), `ambience` (sound), `anchor`, `locks` (what never changes between shots), `lighting[]` and `views[]`.

- `basis` is `design` (stated by the design), `derived` (worked out from the design, say how in `production_assumptions`), `production_assumption` (chosen for the picture) or `unknown` (then `value` is null). Never present an assumption as a design figure.
- A construction hall or paint hall must hold the yacht with working room around it: its length, width and height follow `dimensions` plus clearance. A towing tank holds a scale model; a studio holds desks, screens and people; the sea and an anchorage have no hall dimensions (use `unknown`).

### Step 2 — Interior sets

For every room the script shows give `room`, `identity`, `states[]`, `lighting[]` and `views[]`.

- `identity` holds only what is the same in every state: `geometry` (shape and size of the room), `window` (window type and positions) and `structure` (fixed structural features: ceiling shafts, columns, stairs). Never put finishes, colours, furniture or decorative details in `identity`.
- `states[]` holds every state the script uses for that room (from `beats[].rooms` and the tour), each with `anchor`, `materials`, `palette` (hex, the colours of that state: primer and insulation for bare, the finished palette for finished) and `detail` (the room\'s signature detail when that state shows it, else null). You may add another state only when a reference image needs it; never drop a used state.
- `bare`: the room\'s own structure in the build state\'s materials, with temporary work lights, no finishes and no furniture. `fitout`: linings and joinery installed, floors under protective boards, furniture wrapped in white film. `finished`: the room as `anchors.interior` states it.
- The geometry, window positions and structure never change between states. `identity` is sent to the image model next to every state anchor, so a state anchor must never contradict it; a `bare` or `fitout` anchor describes only what that state shows.

### Step 3 — Exterior amenities

For every exterior zone the script shows (tour stops `EXTxx`, beats that show a yacht exterior area) give `zone`, `anchor` (from `anchors.exterior`, the same area), `operating` (the operational condition from `canonical.operational_limits` when a feature is shown working, else null), `setting` (the `LOC` id it sits in, or null), `lighting[]` and `views[]`.

### Step 4 — Light and views

Each space has one or more `lighting[]` entries; light does not create a new space. Each entry: `id` (`L1`, `L2`…, unique inside its space), `time`, `key` (one main source: `source`, `direction`, `kelvin` as an integer), `practicals[]` (secondary sources with `kelvin`) and `description` (one sentence an image model can follow).

Each space also has one or more `views[]`: `id` (`V1`, `V2`…, unique inside its space) and `camera` (one sentence: where the camera stands, what it looks at, the lens width). Two views of one space are two deliberate camera positions; requests that must match frame by frame use the same view.

### Step 5 — Bindings

`bindings[]` ties the script to the spaces. One entry per space a beat shows: `beat`, `tour_stop` (the stop index for B14 tour stops, else null), `part` (which part of the beat), `space` (`kind`, `id`, `state` — the room\'s state for `interior`, null otherwise), `lighting` (an id of that space) and `view` (the view of that space the beat needs).

- Every beat has at least one binding; a beat that shows several spaces has one binding per space.
- Every room and state in `beats[].rooms` is bound in its beat; every tour stop is bound with its index and its own id.

### Step 6 — Reference requests

`reference_requests[]` lists the images to make: `id` (`R01`…), `space`, `lighting`, `view` (an id of that space\'s `views[]`), `purpose` and `inputs[]`.

- Every binding has a request with exactly its space, state, lighting and view: a beat in sodium night light and a beat in daylight in the same hall need two requests. Only those requests and their source images are required; any other request is optional support. Do not add requests for rooms, states, lights or views the script never shows.
- A second light of the same space is best made from the first one: take it as a `keep_view` input.
- `inputs[]` names source images, each with a `role`:
  - `{"kind": "request", "id": "R03", "role": "keep_view"}`: an image of the same space and the same view whose camera, framing, geometry and windows must be kept; at most one, always listed first.
  - `{"kind": "request", "id": "R03", "role": "identity"}`: an image of the same space in another state or view, used only for its materials, shapes and colours, never for its camera.
  - `{"kind": "request", "id": "R05", "role": "setting"}`: the location an exterior amenity sits in, used only as surroundings.
  - `{"kind": "model_sheet", "id": null, "role": "identity"}`: the approved model sheet.
  - At most four inputs.
- An exterior request always takes the model sheet as an `identity` input; its `setting` input, if any, is a request of the amenity\'s `setting` location.
- When a room is shown in more than one state, the requests of that room share one `view` and every request but one takes another request of the same room as its `keep_view` input (render one state, then edit it into the others). A tour stop with `match_bare_beat` repeats that beat\'s camera: the tour stop\'s binding uses the same `view` as the binding of the same room in `bare` in that beat.
- Never write file names, artifact ids, prices or approvals: the system creates and tracks the images.

### Anchors

Every `anchor` (location, interior state, exterior amenity): not empty, at most 250 words, English, space type → size → surfaces → fixed equipment → light → atmosphere. Write as much as the space needs; there is no minimum. No camera words (cameras live in `views[]`), no people for interiors. A `location` anchor describes the place, never the yacht\'s design.

Wrong:

```
A huge epic shipyard at night with dramatic cinematic lights
```

Right:

```
Enclosed construction hall, 230 m long and 62 m high, grey epoxy floor, two yellow overhead gantry cranes on rails, scaffolding towers along both walls, sodium high-bay lights casting warm top-down light, daylight shafts through roof panels, faint welding haze.
```

