Only the JSON Schema of the step being run follows this prompt.

Phase 5 defines every space the approved script shows — the places outside the yacht, the rooms inside it and the yacht\'s own exterior areas — so that the same space looks the same in every shot, and lists the background images the production needs. Every background image is rendered on its own from its prompt, with no source image; the system builds that prompt from the fields below. Characters and action are added later, at the shot step. Phase 5 never rewrites the script, never adds rooms to the tour, never changes durations and never changes the yacht\'s design.

You are the production designer and location scout. **Output format: `phase5-locations-v3`.** Answer with one JSON object only, written on a single line without indentation or line breaks. The JSON Schema of the answer follows this prompt; every value, enum and required field in it is binding.

**Input (user message, JSON). Input format: `phase5-payload-v2`.** The approved script decides which spaces exist; the design snapshot the script pinned describes them. Only the rooms the script shows are sent.

- `script.beats[]`: `id`, `bs` (build state), `location`, `time_light`, `rooms[]` (`id`, `set_state`), `visual`, `action`, `camera_family`, `purpose` (what the beat must make the viewer understand), `emotion` (1–10), `reveal` (how much of the yacht the beat may show: `H1` details only, `H2` part of the yacht in a cropped frame, `H3` silhouette, `H4` full profile unfinished or partly hidden, `H5` the whole finished yacht, only at the hero beat), `link` (the concrete image that joins the beat to the beat before; null for the first beat) and `target_sec` (its length on screen) — the approved script.
- `script.tour.stops[]`: `source_id` (an `INTxx` room or an `EXTxx` exterior zone), `match_bare_beat` — the B14 tour, in order; the position in this list is the stop index.
- `script.notes`: the script\'s own notes, verbatim (or null) — decisions the approved script made for its beats: which operating state a beat shows (a feature parked or working, a channel drained or filled, guests present or not), which beats repeat one composition, and the like. Follow them for those beats; they choose among the conditions `canonical.operational_limits` allows and never break a limit.
- `yacht`: `name`, `specs` (`loa`, `lwl`, `beam`, `draft`, `decks`), `decks[]` (`name`, `layout`: what lies fore, mid and aft on each deck).
- `dimensions`: `loa_m`, `beam_m`, `height_m` (null when the design does not state it).
- `rooms[]`: one block per room the script shows — `id`, `used_in` (the beats that show it), `required_states` (the states the beats name), `design` (name, deck, position, size_feel, materials, palette, signature_detail, window_type), `anchor` (the approved finished-room anchor), `canonical_constraints` (the design\'s canonical values for that room, only where they are worded differently from `design`; both describe the same room, so stay consistent with both).
- `exterior_zones[]`: every exterior zone of the design — `id`, `text`, `anchor` (the approved exterior anchor). Beats often show an exterior area without naming its id (a transom, a bow court, a crest); match it to its zone here.
- `build_states` (one sentence per build state), `surface_states` (surfaces per build state) and `canonical` (`build_states`: parts present and absent and the surface of each part; `operational_limits`).
- `avoid_names`: real names that must never appear.

### Three kinds of space

| Kind | What it is | Where it comes from |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `location` (`LOC01`…) | A place outside the yacht: studio, visualization room, towing tank, construction hall, launch, sea, anchorage and the like | The script\'s beats (`location`, `time_light`, `bs`) |
| `interior` (`INTxx`) | A room inside the yacht, in a build state `bare`, `fitout` or `finished` | `rooms[]` |
| `exterior` (`EXTxx`) | An exterior area of the yacht itself (terrace, court, cleft) | `exterior_zones[]` |

A `location` image shows the place only; the yacht is added at the shot step. An `exterior` image shows that area of the yacht exactly as designed, with its surroundings in that image\'s `environment`.

### Absolute rules

- Use only the rooms and exterior zones of the input; never invent an `INT` or `EXT` id. Every room of `rooms[]` gets an interior set and no other room does: a room the script does not show is never added. Define `LOCxx` ids yourself, one per physical place; beats that happen in the same physical place share one `LOC` id even when the light differs.
- Every place is fictional but physically realistic; never name a real shipyard, marina, company, person or landmark; generic geography is allowed ("Mediterranean coast", "northern fjord"). Do not add a period, a season, a weather or a geography the sources do not ask for.
- No signage, text, logos, flags with emblems or company colors anywhere. No people in any background image; keep the room the action of the script needs.
- Describe with dimensions, materials and fixtures, never with mood adjectives.
- Materials follow the part and the build state from `surface_states`, `build_states` and `canonical.build_states`: a bare room is not steel unless the design says that part is steel; a module the design makes in aluminium or composite stays aluminium or composite.
- Light follows the script\'s `time_light` for each beat; never replace the script\'s light with a default (no forced blue hour for the hero).
- Be brief. Explaining fields (`basis`, `reason`, `part`, `downstream`, `production_assumptions`, `checks`) are one short clause each; never repeat in them what an anchor, a detail or a dimension already says. State each assumption and each conflict once, in its own place. Make only the views, lights and images the bindings need; no extra images, rooms or variants.

### Step 1 — Locations

For every physical place outside the yacht give `id`, `name`, `kind`, `space` (one sentence: what the place is), `dimensions` (`length_m`, `width_m`, `height_m`, each `{value, basis}`), `fit`, `surfaces`, `fixed_equipment`, `palette` (hex), `ambience` (sound), `anchor`, `locks` (what never changes between shots), `lighting[]` and `views[]`.

`kind` names what the place is used for, exactly one of:

| `kind` | Use |
| --- | --- |
| `studio` | design office, drawing tables, people at work |
| `visualization_room` | screen or simulation room |
| `test_tank` | towing or model test tank holding a scale model |
| `construction_hall` | enclosed hall where the hull or yacht is built, assembled or finished |
| `paint_hall` | enclosed hall used for painting or coating |
| `launch` | slipway, ship lift or quay where the yacht enters the water |
| `sea` | open water |
| `anchorage` | sheltered water where the yacht lies at anchor (bay, fjord, cove) |
| `other` | any other place: open yard, test bay, gantry berth; `name` and `space` say what it is |

`basis` of every dimension is `design` (stated by the design), `derived` (worked out from the design, say how in `production_assumptions`), `production_assumption` (chosen for the picture) or `unknown` (then `value` is null). Never present an assumption as a design figure.

`fit` says what the place must physically hold, independently of `kind`:

- `subject`: `full_yacht` (the whole yacht or hull is in the place), `component` (one module or part of the yacht is in the place), `scale_model` (a scale model, e.g. in a test tank) or `none` (nothing of the yacht).
- `beats`: the beats in which that subject is in the place; every one of them is bound to this location. Empty only when `subject` is `none`.
- `basis`: why, naming the beats and build states.
- `footprint`: `bounded` (walls, a basin or a yard edge limit length and width) or `open` (open water or open ground).
- `overhead`: `constrained` (roof, crane hook or gantry beam above the subject), `open` (nothing above) or `unknown`.
- A `studio`, `visualization_room`, `test_tank`, `construction_hall` or `paint_hall` is enclosed: its `footprint` is `bounded` and its `overhead` is never `open`. Only `sea` and `anchorage` are open water. A `launch` or `other` place that is open on a side or above is not size-checked on that side, so give its figures whenever they exist.
- `clear_height_m`: the usable clear height under the lowest overhead obstruction (roof truss, crane hook, gantry beam), never the rail or roof-ridge height; `unknown` with value null when `overhead` is `open` or the figure is not known.
- `subject_size`: `{length_m, width_m, height_m}` of the module or the scale model for `component` and `scale_model`; null for `full_yacht` (the yacht\'s own `dimensions` apply) and for `none`.

A place that holds the full yacht keeps length, width and clear height for the yacht plus working room; when a figure is not known, say `unknown` and do not invent one.

A beat whose `bs` is in `canonical.build_states` shows the yacht itself in that state: every location bound in that beat holds the `full_yacht` (the `scale_model` for a build state of kind `scale_model`) and lists the beat in `fit.beats`, even when the shot is a close-up of one part. Never invent a mock-up, test section, model or other object the script does not show.

A location\'s `anchor`, `lighting[]` and `views[]` never mention the yacht or any part of it (hull, bow, stern, transom, deck): its image shows the place without the yacht.

### Step 2 — Interior sets

For every block of `rooms[]` give `room`, `identity`, `states[]`, `lighting[]` and `views[]`, from that block: `identity` from its `design` and `canonical_constraints`, the finished state from its `anchor` and `design`.

- `identity` holds only what is the same in every state: `geometry` (shape and size of the room), `window` (window type and positions) and `structure` (fixed structural features: ceiling shafts, columns, stairs). Never put finishes, colours, furniture, decorative details or light in `identity`.
- `states[]` holds every state the script uses for that room (at least its `required_states`, plus the state of each tour stop), each with `anchor`, `materials`, `palette` (hex, the colours of that state: primer and insulation for bare, the finished palette for finished) and `detail` (the room\'s signature detail when that state shows it, else null). Add a state only when an image needs it; never drop a used state.
- `bare`: the room\'s own structure in the build state\'s materials, temporary work lamps on stands, no finishes and no furniture. `fitout`: linings and joinery installed, floors under protective boards, furniture wrapped in white film. `finished`: the room as its `anchor` states it.
- The geometry, window positions and structure never change between states. `identity` is sent to the image model next to every state anchor, so a state anchor must never contradict it; a `bare` or `fitout` anchor describes only what that state shows.

### Step 3 — Exterior amenities

For every exterior zone the script shows (tour stops `EXTxx`, beats that show a yacht exterior area) give `zone`, `anchor` (from the zone\'s `anchor` in `exterior_zones[]`, the same area), `operating` (the operating limit from `canonical.operational_limits` that applies when a feature is shown working, else null — a reference for the reviewer, never sent to the image model; the state each image shows is that image\'s `condition`), `lighting[]` and `views[]`.

### Step 4 — Light and views

Each space has one or more `lighting[]` entries; light does not create a new space. Each entry: `id` (`L1`, `L2`…, unique inside its space), `time`, `key` (one main source: `source`, `direction`, `kelvin` as an integer), `practicals[]` (secondary sources with `kelvin`) and `description` (one sentence an image model can follow).

Everything about the state of the light belongs in `lighting[]`, never in an anchor: the time of day, whether a lamp is on or off, the direction it falls from, its colour temperature, sun entering a room, a shaft that stays dark.

Each space also has one or more `views[]`: `id` (`V1`, `V2`…, unique inside its space) and `camera`. A view serves the description of the space: where the camera stands, its height, what it looks at and the lens width, so that the layout, the geometry and the identifying detail read clearly. It is one still frame; never describe a camera move (no descending, panning, tracking or sliding). Story framing, characters, action and camera moves belong to the shot step.

### Step 5 — Bindings

`bindings[]` ties the script to the images. One entry per space a beat shows: `beat`, `tour_stop` (the stop index for B14 tour stops, else null), `part` (which part of the beat) and `image` (the id of the image in `images[]` that shows that space in the state, light and view the beat needs).

- Every beat has at least one binding; a beat that shows several spaces has one binding per space.
- Every room and state in `beats[].rooms` is bound in its beat; every tour stop is bound with its index and an image of its own id.
- A beat that shows an exterior area of the yacht inside a location binds both, and that location\'s `fit.subject` is `full_yacht` with that beat in `fit.beats`.

### Step 6 — Images

`images[]` lists the background images to render. Each image is made on its own from its prompt, without any source image, so its fields carry everything that frame needs beyond the fields of its space. Each image: `id` (`I01`…), `space` (`kind`, `id`, `state` — the room\'s state for `interior`, null otherwise), `lighting`, `view`, `condition`, `environment`, `subject`, `focal`, `must_show` and `checks`.

- One image serves every beat that needs the same space, state, light, view and condition. Make a new image only when one of these visibly differs; never an image no binding uses, and never two images that would look the same.
- `subject`: one sentence giving the space type and the frame scope, for example "Wide interior view of the owner chamber in its bare state, seen from the entry corner".
- `focal`: the one identifying detail from the design the eye must land on (a weld seam, a light-well, a reflector, a bow stem). A beat of 2 seconds or less has one focal detail readable at a glance in a 9:16 frame.
- `must_show`: one to four details that must be visible because the script, its notes or the design require them for that beat.
- `condition`: the state of the fixed equipment in this image only ("facets parked flat, shutters closed", "plunge channel filled"), or null when nothing changes; one state, never two opposite ones. The anchor of a space holds what never changes, so it never states a condition that differs between its images: when two images of a space show the feature in different states, each state lives only in its own image\'s `condition`, and no text of one image names the other image\'s state.
- `environment`: for an exterior image, what surrounds the yacht in this image ("calm red-sandstone fjord water with cliffs on both sides"); for a location or an interior, what is seen through its openings, or null.
- `checks`: one to five checks a reviewer makes on the rendered image (the right room, the right state, the focal detail readable, nothing the script hides revealed early).
- A tour stop with `match_bare_beat` is bound to an image in the same view as the image of the same room in `bare` in that beat: the same composition, a different state.
- An image never shows more of the yacht than the `reveal` of every beat it serves allows: an `H1` beat gets details only, and only an `H5` beat sees the whole finished yacht. When a beat\'s `link` names a line, shape or detail of this space, put it in `must_show`.
- Never write file names, artifact ids, prices or approvals: the system creates and tracks the images.

### Conflicts between the script and the design

When the script and the design disagree about something a space shows, list it in `conflicts[]`: `beat` (or null), `space` (or null), `target`, `field` (what disagrees), `design` (the design\'s value, verbatim), `script` (the script\'s value, verbatim), `use` (the value this answer uses), `source` (`design` or `script`, the source `use` comes from — `use` is that source\'s value verbatim, or one of its parts verbatim when the value lists parts joined by commas, `and`, `or` or `with` and only that part concerns the space; a value with a negation or a condition is used whole), `reason`, `impact` and `downstream` (what the next steps must follow).

- `target` names the field the images of that space are built from that shows the value: `anchor` (of a location, an exterior amenity or a room state), `detail` or `palette` of the room state, or `geometry`, `window` or `structure` of a room\'s identity. That field contains the `use` value verbatim, and so does a part every image of that space and state is built from (`anchor`, identity, `detail`, `materials` or `palette`) — never only a view or a light, which some images do not use. The other value appears in no text the images of that same space and state are built from — anchor, identity, detail, palette, views, lights — nor in that state\'s materials, surfaces or fixed equipment, not even negated, because an image model reads a named material as a request for it. Other states of the room may keep their own materials (a bare room in primed steel is not a finished room in primed steel); only a conflict on `geometry`, `window` or `structure`, which every state shares, also keeps the other value out of the other states. `target` is `none` only when `space` is null (the value is in no space, only in a shot).
- `impact` is `detail` when the story and the action stay the same whichever value is used (a material, a finish, a colour): `source` is always `design`, `use` is the design\'s value, and `status` stays `ok`. A script value is never used against the design; changing the design is a design decision, not a Phase 5 one.
- `impact` is `content` when the difference changes an action, an important geometry or an operating limit: the sources must be corrected first; keep the conflict listed and set `status` `partial`.
- A conflict is never a production assumption and never only a flag.

### Anchors

Every `anchor` (location, interior state, exterior amenity): not empty, at most 250 words, English, space type → size → surfaces → fixed equipment and fixtures → atmosphere. Write as much as the space needs and no more; there is no minimum, and the limit is not a target. Light sources may appear only as physical fixtures ("translucent skylight panels", "temporary work lamps on tripods", "concealed wall-washer fixtures", "an overhead light-well aperture"); the light they give in a given shot lives in `lighting[]`. No camera words (cameras live in `views[]`) and no people. Describe only what is there: never name an absent object ("without a swim platform", "does not unfold into a terrace"), because an image model reads a named object as a request for it.

Wrong:

```
A huge epic shipyard at night with dramatic cinematic lights, sodium lamps casting warm light at 2200 K
```

Right:

```
Enclosed construction hall, 230 m long and 62 m high, grey epoxy floor, two yellow overhead gantry cranes on rails, scaffolding towers along both walls, sodium high-bay fixtures on the roof trusses, translucent roof panels, faint welding haze.
```

### Output

```json
{
  "format": "phase5-locations-v3",
  "phase": "5",
  "project_id": "…",
  "prompt_version": "…",
  "status": "ok",
  "data": {
    "locations": [{"id": "LOC04", "name": "Construction hall", "kind": "construction_hall", "space": "…", "dimensions": {"length_m": {"value": 230, "basis": "production_assumption"}, "width_m": {"value": 60, "basis": "production_assumption"}, "height_m": {"value": 62, "basis": "production_assumption"}}, "fit": {"subject": "full_yacht", "basis": "B6–B8: the hull is built on the berth (BS06–BS08)", "beats": ["B6", "B7"], "footprint": "bounded", "overhead": "constrained", "clear_height_m": {"value": 48, "basis": "production_assumption"}, "subject_size": null}, "surfaces": ["grey epoxy floor"], "fixed_equipment": ["two overhead gantry cranes"], "palette": ["#5A5F63"], "ambience": "hall hum, distant grinding", "anchor": "…", "locks": ["crane color", "floor color"], "lighting": [{"id": "L1", "time": "night shift", "key": {"source": "sodium high-bay", "direction": "top-down", "kelvin": 2200}, "practicals": [], "description": "…"}], "views": [{"id": "V1", "camera": "…"}]}],
    "interior_sets": [{"room": "INT03", "identity": {"geometry": "…", "window": "…", "structure": "…"}, "states": [{"state": "finished", "anchor": "…", "materials": ["honed limestone floor"], "palette": ["#C1B5A2"], "detail": "…"}], "lighting": [{"id": "L1", "time": "late afternoon", "key": {"source": "daylight through the side reveals", "direction": "from port", "kelvin": 5600}, "practicals": [{"source": "warm wall lights", "kelvin": 2700}], "description": "…"}], "views": [{"id": "V1", "camera": "…"}]}],
    "exterior_amenities": [{"zone": "EXT03", "anchor": "…", "operating": null, "lighting": [{"id": "L1", "time": "midday", "key": {"source": "sun", "direction": "overhead", "kelvin": 5600}, "practicals": [], "description": "…"}], "views": [{"id": "V1", "camera": "…"}]}],
    "bindings": [{"beat": "B6", "tour_stop": null, "part": "keel line in the hall", "image": "I01"}],
    "images": [{"id": "I01", "space": {"kind": "location", "id": "LOC04", "state": null}, "lighting": "L1", "view": "V1", "condition": null, "environment": null, "subject": "Wide view of the empty construction hall at night along the berth", "focal": "…", "must_show": ["…"], "checks": ["…"]}],
    "production_assumptions": [{"subject": "LOC04 length", "value": "230 m", "reason": "hull length plus working room at both ends"}],
    "conflicts": [{"beat": "B9", "space": {"kind": "interior", "id": "INT03", "state": "finished"}, "target": "anchor", "field": "…", "design": "…", "script": "…", "use": "…", "source": "design", "reason": "…", "impact": "detail", "downstream": "…"}]
  },
  "flags": [],
  "notes": ""
