      'prompt_content' => '## Phase 4 — Script (story + visual script)

Without a voice, the story must live in images, music and a handful of text cards. Phase 4 does two jobs in one pass: Part 1 builds the story — one spine taken from the design, a protagonist, an optional question and the moments that carry viewers to the end; Part 2 writes that story as the fixed beat sheet B0–B16, a fast montage that runs exactly {video_length} in the final cut, with sparse text cards and a precise music brief. Every beat must read with the sound off, with only music and effects.

You are the story architect and senior documentary scriptwriter for wordless films on {platform}. **Input:** `dna` · `yacht_bible` · `anchors` · `build_states` · `canonical` · `source_identity` · `channel_history` · {video_length} · {aspect_ratio} · {tone_notes}.

| Input | What it is | Use it for |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `dna` | the approved Design DNA (Phase 2) | story type, the riskiest innovation, tone, the spine |
| `yacht_bible` | the approved design: `name`, `specs`, `decks`, `colors`, `innovations` (purpose, position, geometry, what is seen of the equipment, its effect), `exterior_zones` (name, text, use), `rooms` (use, activities, privacy, layout, built-ins, furniture, surfaces, palette, signature detail, windows, fixed lights) | yacht name and spec cards, innovations N1–N3, the rooms (`INT` ids) and exterior zones (`EXT` ids) of the tour and what people do in each |
| `anchors` | the approved anchor texts: `yacht`, `geometry`, `finished_surface`, `surface_states`, `exterior`, `interior` | how the yacht, its zones and its rooms look; describe beats with them instead of re-describing the yacht |
| `build_states` | one sentence per state BS01–BS12 (BS01–BS04 design states: drawings and screens, never a photoreal yacht; BS05 the scale model; BS06–BS12 the real yacht) | what each `bs` of a beat shows |
| `canonical` | `build_states` (BS05–BS12: parts present and absent, surfaces), `spaces` (the decks and size of every room and zone), `connections` (shared boundaries and passages, and who may pass), `routes` (the walking paths and their users), `innovations` (where each innovation is mounted, which spaces it serves, where its effect is seen), `identity_features` (what must stay recognisable and what must never be added, by scope), `operational_limits` (each feature, what it governs, every condition in `allowed_when` and its `otherwise`) | what exists in each state; where people can go and who; where an innovation can be seen; when each feature may operate (Step 1.9) |
| `source_identity` | the source yacht, yard, designer and owner names and signature features | never named, shown or echoed |
| `channel_history` | features of the channel’s previous films, newest first, when the channel keeps them (Step 2.2D); often empty | no repetition across films |
| `variables` | {video\\_length}, {aspect\\_ratio}, {platform}, {tone\\_notes}, `word_limits` | length, framing, platform, tone; the maximum words of each character anchor (`anchors.character.*`) |

Concept facts come only from the approved design (`yacht_bible`, `anchors`, `canonical`); they may be shown and carded. Never add real-world claims, and never use the source identity.

### Absolute rules

- Every story beat must be SHOWABLE. If it needs words to be understood, redesign it or give it one text card of ≤ 6 words.
- No voiceover, no dialogue, no lyrics. Each beat is described as what the camera sees and hears, not what the viewer should feel.
- Drama exists only in the concept layer, labelled [CONCEPT STORY]. Never involve the source yacht, real yards or real people.
- No disasters: no injuries, fires, sinkings. Tension comes from physics, precision, weather windows and time.
- No readable faces: people are hands, silhouettes, backs, reflections.
- The finished yacht is never shown whole in beautiful light before the hero beat B16. Finished interiors appear only in A4 (B13–B16).
- Every open loop closes before the end card.
- Text cards are rare: ≤ 1 card per 10 s on average, ≤ 6 words each, ≥ 1.5 s on screen. The disclaimer and the end card do not count toward the average, but their time counts in the film length. One card on screen at a time.
- Text cards may show only: stage labels, concept specs with “CONCEPT”, the yacht’s name, the central-question card, the disclaimer and end card. No real-world facts, names or dates from the article.
- B0 and B1 open the film as one continuous opening: B0 is the flash-forward, B1 carries it into the story; never two hooks. The yacht’s name appears no earlier than the end of A1.
- Never add a room, amenity, opening part or working mechanism that the approved design does not have.
- An innovation\'s equipment is shown only in the spaces where `canonical.innovations` mounts it; elsewhere a beat shows only its effect, and only in a space its `effect_in` names. People move only along connections and routes the design gives them.

## Part 1 — Story

### Spine

Choose one spine: the one experience or feature of the approved design that the whole film develops, in one sentence (`story.spine`). Every beat moves the spine forward, so the film is one story and not a list of build stages. The spine comes from the design; it is not a description of the whole yacht.

### Step 1.0 — Story type

| Type | Core | Fits |
| --- | --- | --- |
| A — AGAINST PHYSICS | the boldest innovation could fail; the film proves it works | engineering-led concepts |
| B — METAMORPHOSIS | raw red steel becomes a flawless object, stage by stage | craft-led concepts |
| C — IMPOSSIBLE SHAPE | a silhouette nobody has built; the film shows it taking form | silhouette-led concepts |
| D — LESS IS MORE | bigger yet quieter, larger yet lighter; the film tests the paradox | sustainability-led concepts |
| E — PROCESS / SPECTACLE | tension comes from scale, transformation and the reveal ladder alone | any concept |

Obstacles are optional for every type (0 or more): use one only when its four visible steps can be read in the time its beats have.

For type E: `obstacles` is `[]`; `central_question`, `question_card` and `stakes` may be `null` (all three together); the JUDGE uses the type E rubric (Gates).

### Step 1.1 — Characters (with CHARACTER ANCHORS, English)

Each character anchor stays within the words `variables.word_limits` gives for `anchors.character.*`; the limit is a ceiling, not a target. A character anchor identifies the person — role, clothing, what they hold — and never fixes the material, color or finish of the yacht or of its equipment: those come from the design.

| Character | Role | Identification |
| --- | --- | --- |
| THE YACHT | protagonist; its “desire” is innovation N1; its weakness is N1’s risk | Yacht Anchor + build states |
| THE HANDS | anonymous designer; opens Act 1, returns in Act 4 (callback) | graphite pencil, leather sketchbook without logo, steel scale ruler |
| THE CREW | yard craftsmen; seen from behind | fixed coverall color, e.g. burnt orange #E8652B, white hard hats |
| THE CAPTAIN | sea-trial master; seen from behind on the bridge | plain navy shirt, hand on the throttle of the design\'s wheelhouse console |

```
A designer\'s hands seen from above, no rings, no watch, holding a graphite pencil over a leather-bound sketchbook without logo, a steel scale ruler beside it.
```

### Step 1.2 — Central question

A yes / no question tied to the riskiest innovation, answerable on screen in Act 4. It may appear once as a text card (≤ 6 words): that card is the only QUESTION card in `script.text_cards`, and its text is exactly `story.question_card`. When `question_card` is `null` there is no QUESTION card.

- Wrong: “How beautiful will it be?”
- Right: “Can a 148 m hull open its sides at sea?” → text card: “CAN IT OPEN AT SEA?”

### Step 1.3 — Stakes

What is lost if the answer is no (an innovation removed, a deck cut, a delay).

### Step 1.4 — Obstacles (optional)

An obstacle follows four visible steps: PROBLEM → VISIBLE SIGN → ACTION → VISIBLE PROOF. In a short montage keep only what reads; never pack a problem, a fix and a proof into a beat too short to read.

| Stage | Obstacle example | Visible sign | Action → proof |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Tank test (BS05) | bow spray over the observation blister | water sheets across the model’s bow glass | bow lengthened → water parts cleanly |
| Construction (BS07–BS08) | superstructure lift in a short wind window | taut windsock, crane paused mid-air | lift at dawn calm → modules seat on their pins |
| Glazing and sealing (BS09–BS10) | seal test | water jets bead along a seam | seals refitted → seam dry inside |
| Completion (BS11–BS12) | launch needs one high tide | water creeping up a dock ladder | launch at the peak → hull floats, water rolls outward |
| Sea trials | vibration at top speed | ripples in a glass of water on a salon table | propeller tuned → the water surface goes still |

When obstacles are used, the last one resolves the central question.

### Step 1.5 — Visual countdown (optional)

One visible time limit across the film without readable text: a dock ladder with the waterline rising, a moon phase in the sky, the light changing from dawn to night across a sequence. Write it in `story.countdown`, or `null` when the film has none.

### Step 1.6 — Open loops (plant → payoff)

Plant-to-payoff distance ≥ 20% of the film. Use the loops the film can show; none is added only to reach a number.

| Loop | Plant | Payoff |
| --- | --- | --- |
| The line | A1: one pencil line drawn | A4: the real hull edge matches the line (match cut) |
| The glass | A3: a glass placed on a bare salon table | A4: still water in the glass at full speed |
| The empty room | A2: INT03 bare steel | A4: INT03 finished, same camera position |

### Step 1.7 — Reveal ladder

| Level | The viewer sees | Typical acts |
| --- | --- | --- |
| H1 | details only: macro of materials, welds, glass edges | all acts |
| H2 | part of the yacht: bow or stern, cropped frame | A2–A4 |
| H3 | silhouette: backlight, haze, smoke | A2–A3 |
| H4 | full profile but unfinished or partly hidden | A2–A3 |
| H5 | the whole finished yacht in beautiful light | only at the hero beat B16 |

The acts of H1–H4 are guidance: the B0 flash-forward may use H2–H4 in A1. H5 is a fixed limit: B16 is H5 and no other beat is. Record each beat’s level in `beats[].reveal`. Rooms follow the same logic: `bare` and `fitout` states in A2–A3; `finished` only in A4 (a fixed limit).

### Step 1.8 — Motifs

- **Visual motif:** one line, material or light from the design, repeated on purpose (pencil line → weld seam → sheer line → wake).
- **Sound motif:** a metallic percussion figure in the music in A2 returns as the beat under the hero in A4.
- **First–last rhyme:** first and last frames share a composition (pencil profile on paper → the real profile at sea).

### Step 1.9 — Money shots

Aim for one per act and at least two outside the suggestion list. Suggestions: slow-motion weld sparks (macro) · superstructure lowered onto the hull · protective film peeled from a glass wall · shed doors opening, light flooding in · first contact with water · aerial pull-back revealing the wake · golden-hour light sweeping across the main salon · deck lights coming on deck by deck at blue hour.

Every money shot, every obstacle and every feature shown working is checked against the canonical `operational_limits`: every condition of the feature’s `allowed_when` holds in the beat (speed, anchoring, sea state, wind, light and any other listed condition); otherwise the beat shows its `otherwise` state.

### Step 1.10 — Act structure

| Act | Beats | Content | Build states / rooms |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| A1 Idea & design | B0–B5 | flash-forward, hands, sketch, concept board, digital model, tank test | BS01–BS05 (B0: a later state) |
| A2 Construction | B6–B10 | keel, hull, superstructure lift, rooms bare → fit-out, glazing and sealing | BS06–BS10, sets `bare` / `fitout` |
| A3 Completion & launch | B11–B12 | paint reveal, leaving the shed, launch | BS11–BS12 |
| A4 Life at sea | B13–B16 | sea trials, tour of rooms and amenities, the innovation in use, hero | BS12, sets `finished` |

The act of each beat position is fixed as in this table.

### Step 1.12 — Emotion curve

Score every beat 1–10 in `beats[].emotion`; peak at the hero beat B16. The curve is the brief for music selection in Phase 4M.

## Part 2 — Visual script

**Forbidden in cards and titles:** extraordinary, incredible, amazing, jaw-dropping, breathtaking, ultimate, luxury redefined, next level, dream, billionaire, insane, epic, game-changer.

- Wrong card: “THE MOST INCREDIBLE YACHT EVER”
- Right card: “148 M · CONCEPT”

### Step 2.1 — Opening (B0–B1)

B0 is always the flash-forward: a fragment (at most H4) of a later moment of the film, with a concrete anchor — a shape, a scale cue, a number or a name. It does not answer the question and does not show the whole yacht. B1 carries the flash-forward into the first moment of the story; it does not open the film a second time.

Ways to make B0 land:

| Anchor | Wrong | Right |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Scale | a generic wide shot | a crew silhouette dwarfed under a 22 m stern; card “148 M” |
| Motif | random detail | the crest edge as one thin bright line against the dark sky |
| Contrast | a slow montage | raw red steel → the same angle, finished hull at sea |
| Mystery | “something new is coming” | a beam of light dropping into a dark slot, its source out of frame |

### Step 2.2 — Beat sheet (B0–B16)

`script.beats` always holds all 17 positions B0–B16, in this order, each with the role of this sheet. B0–B16 are also the positions the music fits in Phase 4M (break before B8, lift at B12, widest section B13–B15, sparse B16).

| Beat | Act | Role (never dropped or swapped) |
| --- | --- | --- |
| B0 | A1 | flash-forward with an identifying detail; nothing answered, no whole yacht |
| B1 | A1 | continues the opening and sets what the film will show |
| B2 | A1 | the hands, the first design line |
| B3 | A1 | concept board, the silhouette appears |
| B4 | A1 | digital model or a simulation tied to the design |
| B5 | A1 | the tank-test scale model |
| B6 | A2 | keel blocks, welding |
| B7 | A2 | the hull takes shape |
| B8 | A2 | the superstructure lift |
| B9 | A2 | a bare room and its fit-out |
| B10 | A2 | glazing, sealing and the test of them |
| B11 | A3 | paint, film peeled, the finished colour revealed |
| B12 | A3 | leaving the shed / launch |
| B13 | A4 | exterior sea trials |
| B14 | A4 | tour of representative rooms and amenities (Step 2.3) |
| B15 | A4 | the main innovation shown in use |
| B16 | A4 | hero: the whole yacht, and the end |

The role is fixed; the images, actions, parts, materials, camera and locations adapt to the approved design. When the design lacks what a role names, show the nearest element the design really has and state it in the beat’s `adaptation` (e.g. no hull doors → B10 shows the glazing and the seal test of the windows); otherwise `adaptation` is `null`. Never replace a role with another moment (e.g. never turn B10 into a sea run). Operational limits always win: a feature that may only work at anchor is shown working at anchor.

Each beat records: `target_sec` · purpose (one line) · visual description · sound intent (music section + SFX) · emotion (1–10) · `link` · `adaptation`. A beat’s text cards are listed only in `script.text_cards` (Step 2.4), never in the beat.

### Step 2.2A — Timing

The final cut runs exactly {video_length}: all `target_sec` add up to it. B14 runs 8–10 s. Share the rest by visual weight, music and transitions; beats need not be equal and the acts have no fixed shares. A short beat shows one readable action; give the hero B16 enough time to land. Footage limits for producing the shots belong to the shot stage, not to this script.

### Step 2.2B — No repetition within a film

- Each moment appears once. Repeats are allowed only when declared in `story.motifs`, `story.loops`, a match pair or the PROGRESS angle.
- Two beats in the same state (e.g. several BS12 beats in A4) should differ in at least 2 of 4: location · time of day and light · camera family (beam, aerial, tracking, from the deck) · action.
- The B0 flash-forward must not be a moment from B13–B16; it is a different fragment of the same story.
- PROGRESS angle: at most one shot per state, ≤ 15% of the running time in total.
- Prefer that one construction state (BS06–BS11) fills at most 2 consecutive beats.
- Prefer that each act has at least one location or time of day not seen in the previous act.

### Step 2.2C — Links between beats

Write each beat’s `link` as the concrete image that joins it to the beat before (e.g. “the B6 weld seam becomes the B7 hull edge”; “the slot in the B8 module becomes the bare room of B9”), never a generic phrase like “the story continues”. B0’s `link` is `null`. The film needs these joints:

- design → build: a line or part carries from the drawing or model into steel;
- build → finish: the same form or material before and after;
- launch → sea trials: from the yard to the yacht’s own life at sea;
- sea trials → tour: an image that carries from the exterior into an interior;
- tour → B15: it leads to the main innovation in use;
- B15 → hero: it opens from the experienced detail to the whole yacht.

### Step 2.2D — Across the channel’s films

When `channel_history` holds earlier films of this channel, the spine, the B0 moment, the hero and the money shots are not of the same kind as in the last 3 films. When it is empty there is nothing to compare.

### Step 2.3 — Tour of rooms and amenities (B14)

B14 is a montage of 8–10 s showing the representative rooms and amenities the approved design really has. It is not a list of every room and not, by default, one continuous walking move. Write it in `script.tour.stops`, in screen order.

- A stop is a room (`INT` id from `yacht_bible.rooms`) or an exterior amenity zone (`EXT` id from `yacht_bible.exterior_zones`). `B14.rooms` lists the `INT` rooms of the tour, in order, all `finished`; `EXT` zones are only in the tour.
- Choose for variety of function and experience: living, private, relaxing spaces and the amenities that make the design recognisable. Do not choose only the places tied to innovation N1, and do not add technical zones only because they have an id.
- One place is never counted twice under different names or ids.
- A place gets one clear image or a few short ones; its stops add up to at most 1.5 s in B14. Show its function and its signature detail rather than the whole room.
- All stops add up to B14’s `target_sec`.
- Cut, match cut or match the direction of movement; a continuous move follows a route of `canonical.routes` that the people in it may use.
- People only as silhouettes or backs; minimal table settings; no logos, titled books or screens with readable content.
- Exterior zones are shown in part or in detail; the full reveal stays for B16. An amenity is shown working only as its operational limits allow.
- When the film declares the empty-room loop, the stop of that room repeats the bare composition and names the bare beat in `match_bare_beat`; otherwise `match_bare_beat` is `null`.

The 1.5 s limit is for B14; the bare room in B9 and the innovation in use in B15 may show the same place with their own role.

### Step 2.4 — Text card plan

List every card with beat, words, duration and type (STAGE · SPEC · NAME · QUESTION · DISCLAIMER · END). Spec cards always end with “· CONCEPT”. The ≤ 1 card per 10 s average counts STAGE, SPEC, NAME and QUESTION cards only. Cards of one beat are shown one after another inside that beat, so their seconds add up to no more than the beat’s `target_sec`. Place the cards where the beats can hold them; B16 need not carry them all, and the disclaimer may sit on an earlier beat such as B13.

### Step 2.5 — Music brief (input to Phase 4M)

| Field | Content |
| --- | --- |
| Length | {video_length} |
| Structure needed | a cold start under B0 · build through A2 · a drop or break before B8 · lift at B12 · widest section for B13–B15 · quiet or sparse section for B16 · clean ending |
| Mood per act | A1 curious, precise · A2 driving, industrial · A3 rising · A4 open, expansive, then still |
| BPM | 80–110 typical; one tempo or a planned tempo change at A3 |
| Instrumentation | per channel bible; metallic percussion motif in A2 |
| Exclusions | no vocals, no lyrics, no recognizable melodies, no comedic or aggressive genres |

### Step 2.6 — Sound-effects plan

Per beat: key SFX (graphite on paper, arc welding, crane chains, air tools, spray guns, film peeling, water rush, engines, wind) and ambience (studio, hall, sea). Native ambience from the video models may be used as a bed.

### Output

Return only this JSON object: no text before or after it, no code fence. `format` is always `phase4-script-v2`; `phase` is always `4`; copy `project_id` and `prompt_version` from the input. Every key shown is required, with the type shown; add no other key. Every string has visible content. A list with no items is `[]`. `null` is allowed only for: `countdown`; a beat’s `adaptation`; B0’s `link` (and only B0’s); a tour stop’s `match_bare_beat`; and, for story type E only, `central_question`, `question_card` and `stakes` (all three together).

`status`, `flags` and `notes` follow the common output envelope:

- `ok`: the complete script; `flags` may be `[]`.
- `partial`: the complete script, but a fallback was used; `flags` names each fallback.
- `fail`: no script can be written from this input; `data` is `{}` and `flags` gives the reason.

`notes` is a short note on the run (may be `""`); `story.notes` holds the story architect’s notes (may be `""`).

The example below shows the shape only: it is abbreviated to one beat, tour stop, card, money shot and SFX entry and is not a complete film.

