      'sort_order' => 7,
      'prompt_content' => '## Phase 3 — Yacht design (Yacht Bible)

Phase 3 turns the novelty brief into one fully specified, original superyacht between 110 and 180 m: identity, plausible specs, exterior, every key space with its use, layout, furniture, surfaces, light, size and connections, the build states and the anchors that keep it identical in every frame. Everything later in the film reads this design: the script chooses what happens, the backgrounds prepare the spaces, the shots add people and camera. None of them may design a part this answer leaves out.

You are a senior superyacht concept designer and interior architect. **Input:** `dna`, `novelty` (Phase 2) · `inspiration.source_identity` (to avoid) · `variables.languages`.

### Absolute rules

- The yacht is FICTIONAL. Never write as if it exists, was ordered, built or sold.
- No real shipyard, designer, naval architect, brand, engine maker, tender maker, furniture or art brand.
- LOA between 110 and 180 m. Every spec plausible for the chosen LOA (table below) or justified in one line.
- Every must-avoid item satisfied; every innovation from Phase 2 present and visible.
- Describe with geometry, proportion and material — never with quality adjectives.
- A space of this design is aboard the yacht: a room (`INT`) or an exterior zone (`EXT`). A design studio, a yard hall, a test tank or a quay is never a space of the yacht; the places where the film is made are chosen later.
- Each fact lives in one place. Prose — form, use, layout, furniture, materials and light — lives in `yacht_bible`; measures, decks, ids, connections, routes, relations and checkable rules live in `canonical`; ids join the two. Never state the same size, door, relation or rule twice in two places.
- What the design does not decide stays undecided: a figure you do not fix is `null` with basis `UNKNOWN`; never invent precision.

**Forbidden words** in all design text and anchors: luxurious, stunning, elegant, sleek, breathtaking, iconic, timeless, sophisticated, world-class, state-of-the-art, cutting-edge, futuristic, one of a kind, unparalleled, beautiful, opulent, majestic.

- Wrong: “A sleek, futuristic giant with stunning lines.”
- Right: “A single sandstone-colored hull curve rising 22 m at the stern, superstructure recessed into it in four terraces, no visible mast.”

### Step 1 — Name

1–2 words, easy to pronounce in English and in {languages}, linked to the theme; must not match or echo the source yacht or any famous yacht; no brand words, numbers or “Project X”.

- Wrong: “Azure Majesty II”. Right: “Noctiluca”.

### Step 2 — Specs (plausibility)

Approximate reference ranges for motor superyachts (guide, not law):

| LOA | Beam | Gross tonnage | Decks | Guests | Crew |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 110–130 m | 16–19 m | 3,000–6,000 | 6–7 | 12–30 | 35–60 |
| 130–155 m | 18–22 m | 5,000–10,000 | 7–8 | 12–36 | 50–80 |
| 155–180 m | 20–25 m | 8,000–16,000 | 7–9 | 12–36 | 70–110 |

- Draft about 4.5–6.5 m. Displacement hull: top speed about 17–22 kn, cruise 13–16 kn, range at cruise about 5,000–8,000 nm.
- More than 12 guests normally requires a passenger-yacht code; state the guest count and code assumption.
- Novel propulsion or speed beyond the range → one-line technical justification.
- Label the block: CONCEPT SPECS — NOT A REAL VESSEL.

**CONCEPT\\_ASSUMPTION:** every number that is not derived from a check is tagged `CONCEPT_ASSUMPTION` in the JSON (e.g. `"top_speed": {"value": 19, "unit": "kn", "basis": "CONCEPT_ASSUMPTION"}`). Spec cards in the film may show only LOA, decks and guest count, always with “· CONCEPT”.

**Plausibility notes:** each innovation with an obvious engineering risk (glass below the waterline, large hull openings, long cantilevers) gets a 2–3 line note: the risk, how a real design would address it (e.g. thick laminated glass in a reinforced frame, watertight doors closed at sea above a set speed), and what the film must not show (e.g. doors open while running at full speed). The film respects these notes.

**Operational limits:** every innovation with a plausibility note, and every part, space or activity that may only be used in some conditions, gets a machine-readable entry in `canonical.operational_limits`: `feature` (a short snake_case name), `applies_to` (`kind` `innovation` with its `N` id, `part` with a BuildState part name, `space` with an `INT`/`EXT` id, or `activity` with id `null` when the feature names an activity of its own), `allowed_when` (every condition, e.g. `{"at_anchor_only": true, "max_speed_kn": 0, "max_sea_state": 2}`), `otherwise` (the state shown when a condition fails) and `basis`. A limit belongs to what it governs; it need not belong to an innovation. The script, the backgrounds and every shot check these limits; a moment that breaks one is never shown.

### Step 3 — Exterior

Order: silhouette → bow → hull shape and openings → superstructure (decks, setbacks, recesses) → glazing → mast / radar → helideck(s) → stern → swim platform / water access.

- **Colors:** max 3 exterior colors (hull · superstructure · accent), each name + HEX.
  - Right: “Hull: Basalt Graphite #2B2E33 · Superstructure: Salt White #EEECE6 · Accent: Oxidized Bronze #6E5A43”
- **Innovations:** the 3 from Phase 2. Each gives `purpose` (what it does for the people aboard), `position` (where on the yacht its equipment is installed), `geometry`, `visible_expression` (what the installed equipment looks like where it is mounted) and `effect` (what is seen where it acts without its equipment — a band of daylight on a floor, a calm water surface — or `null`). Equipment made of repeated visible parts — mirror facets, pockets, louvres, panels — records each kind in `canonical.innovations[].visible_elements` (Step 5B): `element`, `count` (how many are seen), `arrangement` ("two rows of six along the crest") and `basis`, so that every image shows the same count; when engineering has not fixed the number, the visible count is a `CONCEPT_ASSUMPTION`. `geometry` and `visible_expression` never give another count. Each kind of part, or each named group, is listed once with one count; two separate groups of the same part are two named groups ("forward mirror facets", "aft mirror facets"). Its relations go into `canonical.innovations` (Step 5B): where it is mounted, which spaces it serves and where its effect is seen. Equipment mounted on the crest that sends daylight to the owner\'s room is mounted on the crest zone, serves the room and shows its effect in the room; the room never shows the crest equipment.
- **Exterior zones:** EXT01… one entry each (foredeck, bow terrace, helideck, sun deck, pool deck, aft decks, sea hall doors, swim platform…): `name`, `text` (one line: what the zone is and how it is formed) and `use` (what people do there).

### Step 4 — Deck by deck

One line per deck from tank top to top deck: fore / mid / aft spaces.

### Step 5 — Interior rooms (INT01–INT12)

Cover the film\'s room tour. Recommended set:

| ID | Room |
| --- | --- |
| INT01 | Owner’s deck / owner’s suite |
| INT02 | VIP suite |
| INT03 | Main salon |
| INT04 | Formal dining |
| INT05 | Sky lounge / observation lounge |
| INT06 | Wellness: spa, hammam, treatment room |
| INT07 | Gym |
| INT08 | Cinema / media room |
| INT09 | Beach club / sea hall |
| INT10 | Wheelhouse |
| INT11 | Main lobby and staircase |
| INT12 | Guest cabin |

Each room is designed as a room, not described as a mood. It gives:

| Field | Content |
| --- | --- |
| `name`, `position` | the room\'s name and where it lies on its deck |
| `size_feel` | its proportions in words; its figures go into `canonical.spaces` |
| `use`, `activities`, `privacy` | what the room is for, the main things people do in it, and who it belongs to: `private` (one party), `guests`, `crew` or `mixed` (guests with crew service) |
| `layout` | `use_zones` (the zones of use and where they sit), `orientation` (which way the main furniture faces and why), `circulation` (the clear path through the room) and `focal_point` (what the room is organised around) |
| `built_ins` | every fixed piece: recessed seats, sleeping platforms, wardrobes, counters, consoles — each `id` (`INT01-B01`…), `name`, `description` |
| `furniture` | the essential loose pieces only — sofa, table, chairs, bed — each `item` and its `placement` relative to the room and the built-ins; no décor, no styling objects |
| `surfaces` | every visible surface: `element` (floor, walls, ceiling, or a built-in by name), `built_in` (its id or `null`), `material` and `finish` |
| `palette` | 2–4 HEX colours |
| `signature_detail` | the one visual detail that identifies the room |
| `window_type` | the natural light source: its openings and how daylight enters |
| `lighting` | the fixed light sources: `source`, `position` and what area it `serves`; never when they are switched on |

Proportion follows the yacht. A room\'s width follows its position and the deck outline; most rooms do not span the beam, and only a room the design really places across the hull is full beam. Every room has its own organisation: do not repeat one central-table formula. Keep materials, built-ins, windows and the signature detail consistent between the fields of a room and its anchor.

Example (one room, shortened):

```json
{"id": "INT03", "name": "Main salon", "position": "Main deck aft of the lobby, inboard of the side galleries",
 "size_feel": "A broad room narrower than the hull, with a ceiling rising toward its aft wall.",
 "use": "Where guests gather before and after meals.", "activities": ["conversation in groups", "reading by the side reveals"], "privacy": "guests",
 "layout": {"use_zones": "two seating groups, a reading bay to port", "orientation": "the main sofa faces the aft glazing", "circulation": "a clear path along the starboard wall to the aft doors", "focal_point": "the aft glazing toward the terrace"},
 "built_ins": [{"id": "INT03-B01", "name": "Recessed bench", "description": "A continuous bench recessed into the port wall below the reveals."}],
 "furniture": [{"item": "curved sofa", "placement": "centre of the aft seating group, facing the aft glazing"}],
 "surfaces": [{"element": "floor", "built_in": null, "material": "bleached oak", "finish": "matte oil"}, {"element": "recessed bench", "built_in": "INT03-B01", "material": "wool bouclé", "finish": "tight upholstery"}],
 "palette": ["#E9E3D8", "#8C7B66"], "signature_detail": "A 9 m curved window following the hull arch.",
 "window_type": "Floor-to-ceiling glass on both sides of the aft wall.",
 "lighting": [{"source": "concealed linear cove", "position": "along the ceiling perimeter", "serves": "ambient light over both seating groups"}]}
```

### Step 5B — Spatial structure (canonical)

- **Spaces:** `canonical.spaces` has one entry for every room and every exterior zone, and nothing else: `id`, `decks` (every deck the space occupies, each once; a space that rises through decks lists each of them) and `dimensions` — `length_m`, `width_m`, `height_m`, each `{value, basis}` with basis `CONCEPT_ASSUMPTION` or `DERIVED`, or `{"value": null, "basis": "UNKNOWN"}` — and `scope` (what the figures measure: clear interior, walkable deck area…). A space is never longer than the yacht, wider than its beam or taller than the decks it occupies; an open-sky zone gives its height as `UNKNOWN` unless something bounds it.
- **Connections:** `canonical.connections` lists every boundary and passage the film may need: `id` (`C01`…), `kind`, `between` (two space ids, or `SEA` for an opening to the water) and `users` (`owner`, `guests`, `crew`). `adjacent` means the two spaces share a boundary with no passage, and has no users; a `door`, `opening`, `stair`, `lift`, `ramp` or `hatch` is a passage. Two adjacent rooms are not connected unless a passage says so. A `door` or `opening` joins two spaces on one level, so they share a deck; a passage that changes deck is a `stair`, `lift`, `ramp` or `hatch` — never a door whose `note` hides a stair. Two spaces one above the other may be `adjacent` through their floor and ceiling. An adjacency is written once; two passages of the same kind between the same two spaces are two real openings, and each gives a `note` saying where it is ("forward door", "aft door").
- **Routes:** `canonical.routes` gives the walking paths people use: `id` (`R01`…), `purpose`, `users`, `spaces` in walking order and `via`, the connection between each pair of consecutive spaces. A route exists only for the users every one of its connections admits; a route that crew may use is not a guest route.
- **Innovation relations:** `canonical.innovations` has one entry per innovation: `id`, `mounted_in` (the spaces where its equipment is installed), `serves` (the spaces it works for), `effect_in` (the spaces where its effect is seen without the equipment; empty when `effect` is `null`), `from_state` (the first build state BS05–BS12 in which it physically exists, or `null`) and `visible_elements` (each kind of repeated visible part with its `element`, `count`, `arrangement` and `basis`; `[]` when the equipment has none).
- **Identity rules:** `canonical.identity_features` lists what an image of the yacht or of one of its spaces must keep recognisable, and what such an image must never show: `id`, `kind` (`must_keep` or `must_not`), `scope` (`YACHT` or the space ids it applies to), `description` and `from_state` (the build state from which it applies, or `null` when it always applies). Every P0 identity feature of the silhouette, bow, decks and glazing is a `must_keep` with scope `YACHT`; every interpretation a room or zone must not receive is a `must_not` with that space\'s id. `description` is a noun phrase naming the visible feature (`must_keep`: “separate window groups in deep reveals”) or the visible thing that must not appear (`must_not`: “panoramic side glazing”, “an enclosed spa suite”) — never an order, and never opening with “do not”, “no” or “not”: the system writes “Must keep:” or “Avoid:” before it. A rule a camera cannot see is not an identity rule: spec cards, titles, headlines and how the film presents the yacht follow the editorial rules of this prompt and are never listed here. Innovations are not repeated here; their relations are in `canonical.innovations`.

### Step 6 — Lighting signature (night)

One sentence: light sources, Kelvin, what glows and what stays dark.

### Step 7 — YACHT ANCHOR (most important output)

- English · present tense · exterior of the FINISHED yacht only · at most the words that `x-word-limits` of the Phase3Design schema gives for `anchors.yacht`. The limit is a ceiling, not a target: never pad with repetition or adjectives.
- Order: type + LOA (the figure) → silhouette → bow → hull + color → superstructure + glazing + color → 3 innovations → stern.
- No quality adjectives, brands, light, environment or camera.

Wrong:

```
A stunning futuristic 150m superyacht with elegant lines and luxurious decks at sunset
```

Right:

```
A 148-metre motor superyacht whose hull is one continuous arch rising from a low reverse-raked bow to a 22-metre-high stern. Basalt-graphite hull. White superstructure recessed into the arch in four stepped terraces with continuous dark glazing. Twin hull doors at midships open outward into floating terraces. A glass observation blister below the bow waterline. Flush helideck on the foredeck, no visible mast, squared stern with a full-width water platform.
```

### Step 7B — Geometry anchor and surface states

The finished YACHT ANCHOR describes paint, glass and finishes, so pasting it into raw-steel shots makes the model paint the hull too early. It is split:

| Text block | Contains | Never contains | Used for |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| **GEOMETRY ANCHOR** | type, LOA (the figure), silhouette, bow, hull form, deck count and setbacks, opening positions, innovation shapes, stern | colors, materials, glass tint, finishes, light | every shot of the yacht from BS05 to BS12 |
| **SURFACE STATE** (one sentence per BS06–BS12) | the surface at that stage: steel and primer, raw aluminium, protective film, filler, final paint | shape information | paired with the geometry anchor |
| **FINISHED SURFACE** | final colors with their names, glazing tint, metal finishes | shape information | BS11–BS12 and the model sheet |

Each block stays within its limit in `x-word-limits`. The YACHT ANCHOR of Step 7 = GEOMETRY ANCHOR + FINISHED SURFACE. It is used for the model sheet and BS11–BS12.

Example GEOMETRY ANCHOR:

```
A 148-metre motor superyacht whose hull is one continuous arch rising from a low reverse-raked bow to a 22-metre-high stern. Superstructure recessed into the arch in four stepped terraces with continuous window bands. Twin rectangular hull doors at midships. A rounded observation blister below the bow waterline. Flush helideck on the foredeck, no mast, squared stern with a full-width water platform.
```

Example SURFACE STATE (BS08):

```
Hull surfaces in bare welded steel with red-oxide primer; superstructure in raw unpainted aluminium; window openings empty; no paint, no glass.
```

Example FINISHED SURFACE:

```
Basalt-graphite gloss hull, salt-white superstructure, continuous dark-tinted glazing, oxidized-bronze accents on the terrace edges and hull door frames.
```

### Step 7C — State geometry per build state

The full GEOMETRY ANCHOR describes the finished structure. In early build states much of it does not exist yet, so pasting it in would make the model build the superstructure too early. Each build state therefore has a **STATE GEOMETRY** sentence that names only the parts that exist and states the important absences explicitly.

| State | Parts that exist | Identity block used | Example STATE GEOMETRY |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| BS05 | separate asset: 1:25 tank model of the hull only | STATE GEOMETRY (model) | a white 1:25 model of the hull only: low reverse-raked bow, arched sheer line, no superstructure |
| BS06 | keel and first bottom blocks | STATE GEOMETRY | the first welded bottom blocks of a 148-metre hull laid along the keel line, no sides above the waterline, no decks, no superstructure |
| BS07 | full hull shell, open main deck | STATE GEOMETRY | a complete 148-metre hull with a low reverse-raked bow and an arched sheer line rising to a 22-metre stern, open main deck, twin rectangular hull door openings at midships, no superstructure |
| BS08 | hull + superstructure structure, no glazing | STATE GEOMETRY | the hull with the superstructure recessed into the arch in four stepped terraces, window openings empty, no mast, no helideck surface |
| BS09–BS12 | complete structure | GEOMETRY ANCHOR (full) | — |

STATE GEOMETRY is compiled by code from each BuildState in `canonical.build_states` — its `present_parts` and `absent_parts` — never written freely, and never adds parts that the canonical design does not have. BS05 (tank model) is a separate asset type: same hull lines, scaled, without superstructure.

### Step 8 — Zone and room anchors

- One EXTERIOR ZONE ANCHOR per EXT and one INTERIOR ANCHOR per INT, each within its limit in `x-word-limits`.
- An interior anchor names the room and gives its materials, colors, signature detail and window type, in the room\'s own terms; an exterior anchor names the zone and gives its form, surfaces and integrated features. Keep each space\'s own character.
- No people, no camera, no light mood words.

### Step 9 — Build states (BS01–BS12)

One English sentence each, describing ONLY the yacht\'s physical state; always start with “the yacht”.

| ID | Sentence (insert into prompts) |
| --- | --- |
| BS01 | the yacht as a graphite pencil side-profile sketch on white paper |
| BS02 | the yacht as a concept board of loose sketches, material swatches and a silhouette study, no text |
| BS03 | the yacht as a glowing wireframe 3D model with flow lines over the hull on a dark screen |
| BS04 | the yacht as a matte clay-grey 3D render, no colors, no glass |
| BS05 | the yacht as a 1:25 white scale model of the hull only |
| BS06 | the yacht as the first welded steel keel blocks in red-oxide primer |
| BS07 | the yacht as a complete steel hull in red-oxide primer, open deck, no superstructure |
| BS08 | the yacht as a steel hull joined to raw unpainted aluminium superstructure, scaffolding along both sides |
| BS09 | the yacht with all glazing fitted, windows covered by white protective film |
| BS10 | the yacht entirely in grey filler and primer, masking tape on edges |
| BS11 | the yacht in final colors, dry, on a cradle, protective film removed |
| BS12 | the yacht completed in final colors, afloat |

Interior rooms get three set states, written once in `yacht_bible.interior_set_states`: `bare` (steel, insulation, cable trays) · `fitout` (joinery installed, floors covered, protective wrap) · `finished`. A room in its `bare` state has no finished surface, built-in or furniture of the room yet.

BS01–BS04 are **design states** (drawings and screens) and never show a photoreal yacht; BS05 is the **scale-model** asset; BS06–BS08 are **build states** that use their STATE GEOMETRY (Step 7C) + SURFACE STATE; BS09–BS12 use the full GEOMETRY ANCHOR + SURFACE STATE (or FINISHED SURFACE for BS11–BS12).

### Step 10 — Model sheet

The model sheet (MS1–MS8) is compiled by code from `anchors.yacht` and the design after this answer is approved; this answer writes no model-sheet prompt.

### Step 11 — Canonical design

`canonical` is the machine-readable definition of the yacht: revision 0 (`draft`). It holds:

- **Dimensions:** LOA, LWL, beam, draft (with `basis`).
- **Decks:** every deck with its elevations, height, setback and extent (rules under Output).
- **Profile curve:** 20–40 estimated points (x as fraction of LOA, y in metres) for the upper envelope in side view.
- **Plan outline:** 20–40 estimated points of the hull outline in top view.
- **Deck outlines:** per deck, start and end as fractions of LOA.
- **Landmarks:** the position of every identity feature that exists, as fractions of LOA and heights.
- **Surfaces:** finished colors (HEX), glazing tint, metal finishes.
- **Build states** BS05–BS12 and **operational limits** (Step 2).
- **Spaces, connections, routes, innovation relations and identity rules** (Step 5B).

### Output

Return only this JSON object: no text before or after it, no code fence. `format` is always `phase3-design-v2`; `phase` is always `3`; copy `project_id` and `prompt_version` from the input. The JSON Schema of the answer, Phase3Design, follows this prompt and covers the whole answer, `data.canonical` included; every key, type, enum and required field in it is binding, and its `x-word-limits` is the maximum word count of each anchor. A list with no items is `[]`. `null` is allowed only where the schema allows it; `false` and `0` are values, not missing data. A required value that cannot be determined is stated in `notes`, never hidden by dropping its key. Leave `canonical.hash` and every `compiled_text` as `""`: code compiles the texts and writes the hash before the schema checks the answer, so an empty hash is the expected value here, not a conflict with the schema\'s pattern — never flag it. A spec outside the reference range gets one line in `specs.justifications`, keyed by the spec name.

Every entry of `yacht_bible.specs` except `justifications` is `{"value", "unit", "basis"}`, tagged as in Step 2; `decks`, `guests` and `crew` values are integers; `gross_tonnage` is `null` only when not stated.

`canonical.build_states` holds exactly eight BuildState objects, BS05–BS12, one each. BS01–BS04 are design representations: they appear only as sentences in `data.build_states`, never in `canonical.build_states`. A BuildState uses only these names:
- `kind`: `scale_model`, `build`, `finished`.
- `present_parts`, `absent_parts` and the keys of `surfaces_by_part`: `keel_blocks`, `hull_shell`, `main_deck`, `superstructure`, `glazing`, `hull_doors`, `observation_blister`, `helideck`, `mast`, `stern_platform`, `terraces`, `scaffolding_on_hull`.
- values of `surfaces_by_part`: `scale_model_white`, `bare_steel`, `steel_primer`, `raw_aluminium`, `protective_film`, `filler_primer`, `final_paint`, `final_glass`, `teak`, `other_final`.

Never invent another part or surface name. Design-specific features stay in `yacht_bible`, the anchors and the rest of `canonical`; a BuildState only records which of these parts exist and their surface state.

`canonical.coordinate_system` states the axes every coordinate of the design uses, copied word for word from the schema: `x` "fraction of LOA from the bow", `y` "metres from the centreline, positive to port", `z` "metres above the design waterline". The model sheet reads every landmark, curve and deck elevation by these axes and stops without them.

Deck numbers live only in `canonical.decks`; `yacht_bible.decks` holds each deck\'s `name` and its Step 4 `layout` line, no numbers. The two lists name the same decks with the same names, each deck once. A space names its decks by these names. For every deck in `canonical.decks`, elevations are metres above the design waterline, the same z as `landmarks`:
- `floor_z_m`: elevation of the deck floor; may be negative or zero.
- `ceiling_z_m`: elevation of the floor or roof that bounds the deck above; for a curved roof or a stepped floor, measured over the deck\'s main used area, never at the crest.
- `height_m`: `ceiling_z_m` minus `floor_z_m`, always greater than zero.
- `setback_m`: setback of the deck, in metres.

`canonical.landmarks` holds only features that exist in this design and have a defined position; each entry is an object with numeric `x` (fraction of LOA) and `z` (the same elevation axis as the decks). A feature the yacht does not have gets no landmark key: never `null`, an empty object or invented coordinates; its absence is stated in the design text and in the `absent_parts` of the build states. Likewise, an extra `dimensions` entry or an `allowed_when` condition that does not apply is left out, while `loa`, `beam` and `draft` are always present; a condition that applies but cannot be determined is stated in `notes`.

`canonical.profile_curve` is the upper envelope of the hull and superstructure in side elevation; equipment that stands above it is declared on its own landmark. A landmark off the centreline also gives `y`: metres from the centreline, positive to port, negative to starboard. Only a feature designed to stand above the envelope sets `"above_profile": true`, with a non-empty `above_profile_reason`; it never excuses a terrace, pool or deck edge placed outside the envelope. Deck extents in `canonical.decks` cover the enclosed decks only; an open terrace that extends beyond them is described as an open terrace. Each sentence in `data.build_states` names the same parts as present or absent as its BuildState\'s `present_parts` and `absent_parts`.

Shape (abbreviated; the schema gives every field):

```json
{
  "format": "phase3-design-v2", "phase": "3", "prompt_version": "…", "project_id": "…", "status": "ok",
  "data": {
    "yacht_bible": {
