### Hand-off

Code checks only what can be checked technically: the schema, ids and references, units and figures, positions, boxes, window boxes and points against the envelope at the coarse level described in Step 4, the required part kinds and sides, every part\'s parent, containing space or support, configurations owned only by parts with one default each, every default and the displayed set valid against the limits, the limit vocabulary, light-source references and states, stage exceptions, subspace instances, opening boxes on all three axes, the eight cameras against the pinned `model_sheet` input with MS8\'s camera complete, `max_chars`, forbidden and source names, the DNA\'s innovation and departure ids and `prompt_version`. Code never grades the prose. It then adds what is derived — the deck count, each deck\'s height and extent, the parts present at each build state, each state\'s geometry and the design hash — and pins the design, the model-sheet presentation and the final prompt together. A design with status `ok` and every rule met goes on. Its `final_prompt` is shown verbatim and sent unchanged when the user renders; the user approves the design through the rendered model sheet.
