### Hand-off

Code checks only what can be checked technically. A broken rule fails the design: the schema, ids and references, the DNA\'s structured fields, boxes with their bounds reversed, points on the wrong side, the axle and wheelbase convention, the seating point against the seat height, the required part kinds and sides, configurations and limits, light-source states, stage exceptions, the eight cameras against the pinned `model_sheet` input, `max_chars`, forbidden words, source names, the make outside `motorcycle.base` and every id or name the final prompt must not hold. A coarse comparison of boxes is a warning shown to the user, never a failure: the wheel height against its rim, the profile against the part boxes, points and mountings near their boxes, the ground clearance against the lowest box. Code never grades the prose and never proves that the motorcycle can be ridden. It then lists the parts present at each build state and computes the design hash. A design with status `ok` and no broken rule goes on, with its warnings shown; its `final_prompt` is shown verbatim and sent unchanged when the user renders, and the user approves the design through the rendered model sheet.
